{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 10, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 22 minutes to cover every 4 miles of the city, even though 5 miles of the city coastline are occupied by a harbor. If the city is 84 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 22 minutes to cover every 4 miles of the city, even though 5 miles of the city coastline are occupied by a harbor. If the city is 84 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine the effective length of the city that the fog bank can cover. The city is 84 miles across, but 5 miles of this are occupied by a harbor. Therefore, the fog bank can only cover 84 - 5 = 79 miles of the city. Next, we need to understand the rate at which the fog bank covers the city. The fog bank covers 4 miles in 22 minutes. We can use this information to find out how long it takes to cover 79 miles. We can set up a proportion to solve for the time it takes to cover 79 miles.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time it takes to cover 79 miles as \\( T \\) minutes. We know that 4 miles take 22 minutes, so we can set up the proportion:\n\\[ \\frac{4 \\text{ miles}}{22 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{79 \\text{ miles}}{T \\text{ minutes}} \\]\nCross-multiplying gives us:\n\\[ 4T = 22 \\times 79 \\]\n\\[ 4T = 1738 \\]\n\\[ T = \\frac{1738}{4} \\]\n\\[ T = 434.5 \\]\nTherefore, it will take 434.5 minutes for the fog bank to cover the whole city.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 434.5, "ground_truth": 462.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 11, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 93 minutes to cover every 2 miles of the city, and the fog's density was measured at 7 grams per cubic meter during the morning. If the city is 24 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 93 minutes to cover every 2 miles of the city, and the fog's density was measured at 7 grams per cubic meter during the morning. If the city is 24 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine the total distance the fog bank needs to cover, which is the length of the city. Then, we need to calculate the volume of fog required to cover this distance, given its density. Finally, we need to determine how long it will take for the fog bank to cover the entire city, given its rate of coverage.\n1. Calculate the total distance the fog bank needs to cover:\n   The city is 24 miles across, and the fog bank needs to cover every 2 miles. Therefore, the total distance is:\n   24 miles / 2 miles per segment = 12 segments\n2. Calculate the volume of fog required to cover the distance:\n   The fog bank's density is 7 grams per cubic meter. We need to convert the distance from miles to meters. There are 1609.34 meters in a mile. Therefore, the total distance in meters is:\n   24 miles * 1609.34 meters/mile = 38624.16 meters\n   The volume of fog required is:\n   38624.16 meters * 7 grams/meter^3 = 269369.12 grams\n3. Calculate the time it will take for the fog bank to cover the entire city:\n   The fog bank takes 93 minutes to cover every 2 miles. Therefore, the time it takes to cover 1 mile is:\n   93 minutes / 2 miles = 46.5 minutes per mile\n   The time it will take to cover the entire city is:\n   46.5 minutes/mile * 24 miles = 1116 minutes\n</think>\n<answer>\nIt will take 1116 minutes for the fog bank to cover the entire city.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 1116.0, "ground_truth": 1116.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 12, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 63 minutes to cover every 13 miles of the city, even though there are 5 lighthouses scattered along the coastline. If the city is 39 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 63 minutes to cover every 13 miles of the city, even though there are 5 lighthouses scattered along the coastline. If the city is 39 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to understand the rate at which the fog bank covers the city. We know that it takes 63 minutes to cover 13 miles. We can use this information to find the rate of fog coverage per mile. Then, we can use this rate to determine how long it will take to cover the entire city, which is 39 miles long.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's start by calculating the rate of fog coverage per mile. We know that it takes 63 minutes to cover 13 miles. Therefore, the rate of fog coverage is:\n\\[ \\text{Rate} = \\frac{63 \\text{ minutes}}{13 \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how long it will take to cover the entire city, which is 39 miles long. We can use the rate we just calculated to find the time it takes to cover 39 miles:\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Distance} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\left( \\frac{63 \\text{ minutes}}{13 \\text{ miles}} \\right) \\times 39 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nNow, let's perform the calculation:\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{63 \\times 39}{13} \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{2457}{13} \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Time} = 189 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, it will take 189 minutes for the fog bank to cover the entire city.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 189.0, "ground_truth": 189.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 13, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 176 minutes to cover every 35 miles of the city, even though 4 miles of the coastline are lined with tall cliffs that slow the fog slightly. If the city is 70 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 176 minutes to cover every 35 miles of the city, even though 4 miles of the coastline are lined with tall cliffs that slow the fog slightly. If the city is 70 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine the effective speed of the fog bank, considering the impact of the cliffs. Then, we need to calculate the total distance the fog bank needs to cover, which is the entire length of the city. Finally, we can use the effective speed to find out how long it will take for the fog bank to cover the entire city.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's start by calculating the effective speed of the fog bank. The fog bank takes 176 minutes to cover 35 miles, but 4 miles of the coastline are slowed down by cliffs. Therefore, the fog bank only covers 31 miles (35 - 4) in 176 minutes. The effective speed of the fog bank is then 31 miles / 176 minutes.\nNext, we need to calculate the total distance the fog bank needs to cover. The city is 70 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge. Since the fog bank only covers 31 miles in 176 minutes, we need to find out how many 31-mile segments are in 70 miles. This can be calculated as 70 miles / 31 miles per segment.\nFinally, we can use the effective speed to find out how long it will take for the fog bank to cover the entire city. We can use the formula: time = distance / speed. In this case, the distance is the total length of the city (70 miles), and the speed is the effective speed of the fog bank (31 miles / 176 minutes).\nLet's calculate the total distance the fog bank needs to cover: 70 miles / 31 miles per segment = 2.258 segments.\nNow, let's calculate the time it will take for the fog bank to cover the entire city: time = 70 miles / (31 miles / 176 minutes) = 70 * 176 / 31 minutes.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 31.0, "ground_truth": 352.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 14, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 312 minutes to cover every 3 miles of the city, even though 5 miles of the city are parks with fewer buildings. If the city is 36 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 312 minutes to cover every 3 miles of the city, even though 5 miles of the city are parks with fewer buildings. If the city is 36 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to consider the total length of the city that the fog bank needs to cover and the rate at which it covers the city. The city is 36 miles long, but 5 miles of it are parks. Therefore, the fog bank needs to cover 31 miles of the city (36 - 5 = 31). We know that the fog bank covers 3 miles in 312 minutes. We can use this information to find out how long it will take to cover 31 miles.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, we need to find the rate at which the fog bank covers the city. We know that it covers 3 miles in 312 minutes. To find the rate, we divide the distance by the time:\nRate = Distance / Time\nRate = 3 miles / 312 minutes\nRate = 1 mile / 104 minutes\nNow that we know the rate, we can find out how long it will take to cover 31 miles:\nTime = Distance / Rate\nTime = 31 miles / (1 mile / 104 minutes)\nTime = 31 * 104 minutes\nTime = 3204 minutes\nTherefore, it will take 3204 minutes for the fog bank to cover the whole city.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 3204.0, "ground_truth": 3744.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 15, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 474 minutes to cover every 91 miles of the city, even though 12 of the city’s buildings are taller than 50 feet. If the city is 91 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 474 minutes to cover every 91 miles of the city, even though 12 of the city’s buildings are taller than 50 feet. If the city is 91 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to consider the effect of the buildings on the fog bank's coverage. The fog bank takes 474 minutes to cover every 91 miles of the city, but 12 buildings are taller than 50 feet, which means they block the fog from passing over them. We need to determine the effective length of the city that the fog bank can cover in 474 minutes, taking into account the buildings.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the effective length of the city that the fog bank can cover as \\( L \\). Since the fog bank takes 474 minutes to cover every 91 miles of the city, we can set up the following proportion:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{L}{91} = \\frac{474}{91}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( L \\):\n\n\\[\nL = 474 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nHowever, we need to account for the 12 buildings that are taller than 50 feet. These buildings block the fog from passing over them, so the effective length of the city that the fog bank can cover is reduced by the length of these buildings. Since the city is 91 miles across, and 12 buildings are taller than 50 feet, we can assume that the buildings are evenly distributed along the city's length. Therefore, the length of each building is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Length of each building} = \\frac{91 \\text{ miles}}{12} \\approx 7.5833 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nThe total length of the buildings is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total length of buildings} = 12 \\times 7.5833 \\approx 91 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nSince the buildings block the fog from passing over them, the effective length of the city that the fog bank can cover is:\n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{effective}} = 91 \\text{ miles} - 91 \\text{ miles} = 0 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nThis means that the fog bank cannot cover any part of the city because the buildings block it completely. Therefore, it will take an infinite amount of time for the fog bank to cover the whole city.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 0.0, "ground_truth": 474.0, "is_correct": false}
