/3 off:\n\\[ 5 \\text{ kg} \\times \\$21/\\text{kg} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 5 \\times \\$14 = \\$70 \\]\n\nSo, the total cost for 15 kilograms of fudge is:\n\\[ \\$210 + \\$70 = \\$280 \\]\n\nLeilani bought 23 kilograms of fudge. We need to calculate the cost for the additional 8 kilograms (23 kg - 15 kg). Since the offer is for 15 kilograms, we can consider the first 15 kilograms as a whole and then calculate the cost for the remaining 8 kilograms at the regular price of $21 per kilogram:\n\\[ 8 \\text{ kg} \\times \\$21/\\text{kg} = \\$168 \\]\n\nAdding the cost of the first 15 kilograms and the additional 8 kilograms:\n\\[ \\$280 + \\$168 = \\$448 \\]\n\nLeilani also bought a mixed bag of sand dollars for $11.25 and 15 postcards at $21.25 each:\n\\[ 15 \\text{ postcards} \\times \\$21.25/\\text{postcard} = \\$318.75 \\]\n\nNow, we calculate the total cost of all items:\n\\[ \\$448 + \\$11.25 + \\$318.75 = \\$778.00 \\]\n\nFinally, we subtract the total cost from the initial $1300:\n\\[ \\$1300 - \\$778.00 = \\$522.00 \\]\n\nTherefore, Leilani has $522.00 left.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 522.0, "ground_truth": 522.0, "is_correct": true}
