We'll use the same spacing of 18 feet between plants. Therefore, the number of plants needed along the length is:\nNumber of plants along the length = (Length of flower bed) / (Space required for each plant) = 154 / 18 = 8.555...\nAgain, we can't have a fraction of a plant, so we'll round up to the nearest whole number. In this case, we need 9 plants along the length.\nNow, we can calculate the total number of plants needed:\nTotal number of plants = Number of plants along the width * Number of plants along the length = 1 * 9 = 9\nIsabella already owns 10 plants, so she doesn't need to buy any additional plants. However, we need to calculate the cost of the plants she needs to buy. Each plant costs $12, so the total cost is:\nTotal cost = Total number of plants * Cost per plant = 9 * $12 = $108\nTherefore, Isabella will spend $108 at the store to fill up her flower bed.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 108.0, "ground_truth": 12.0, "is_correct": false}
