{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 463, "question": "Sunnyside High is holding graduation in their Sports Arena this year which has space for 8500 people. After accounting for 700 seats for graduates and 100 seats for faculty attending, and noting that 50 of the graduates requested wheelchair-accessible seating, how many tickets would each graduate receive to give to their friends and family if the tickets are split equally?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Sunnyside High is holding graduation in their Sports Arena this year which has space for 8500 people. After accounting for 700 seats for graduates and 100 seats for faculty attending, and noting that 50 of the graduates requested wheelchair-accessible seating, how many tickets would each graduate receive to give to their friends and family if the tickets are split equally?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of seats available for graduates and faculty.\n2. Subtract the seats reserved for graduates and faculty from the total capacity to find the remaining seats.\n3. Subtract the wheelchair-accessible seats from the remaining seats to find the seats available for regular graduates.\n4. Determine the number of tickets each regular graduate can receive by dividing the remaining seats by the number of regular graduates.\n5. Calculate the number of tickets each graduate can give to their friends and family.\n\nLet's go through these steps one by one:\n\n1. Total seats available: 8500\n   Seats for graduates: 700\n   Seats for faculty: 100\n   Total seats for graduates and faculty: 700 + 100 = 800\n\n2. Remaining seats after accounting for graduates and faculty:\n   8500 - 800 = 7700\n\n3. Subtract the wheelchair-accessible seats:\n   7700 - 50 = 7650\n\n4. Number of regular graduates:\n   700 - 50 = 650\n\n5. Number of tickets each regular graduate can receive:\n   76