{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 640, "question": "Emma has 3 jars of icing in her kitchen cabinet, but 2 of the jars were purchased last week and the third one a month ago. Each jar of icing can decorate 11 brownies. Emma wants to bake enough brownies to use up all of her icing. If each baking sheet holds 11 brownies, how many baking sheets worth of brownies should she bake?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Emma has 3 jars of icing in her kitchen cabinet, but 2 of the jars were purchased last week and the third one a month ago. Each jar of icing can decorate 11 brownies. Emma wants to bake enough brownies to use up all of her icing. If each baking sheet holds 11 brownies, how many baking sheets worth of brownies should she bake?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine the total amount of icing Emma has and then figure out how many brownies she can bake with that amount. We'll break it down into steps:\n1. Calculate the total amount of icing:\n   - 2 jars purchased last week: 2 jars * 11 brownies/jar = 22 brownies\n   - 1 jar purchased a month ago: 1 jar * 11 brownies/jar = 11 brownies\n   - Total icing: 22 brownies + 11 brownies = 33 brownies\n2. Determine how many baking sheets are needed:\n   - Each baking sheet holds 11 brownies\n   - Total brownies to be baked: 33 brownies\n   - Number of baking sheets needed: 33 brownies / 11 brownies/baking sheet = 3 baking sheets\n</think>\n<answer>\nEmma should bake 3 baking sheets worth of brownies to use up all of her icing. </answer>", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 3.0, "is_correct": true}
