al.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 150.0, "ground_truth": 249.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 694, "question": "On Tuesday, Tariq wants to exercise for eight times the amount of time he did on Monday and Sunday combined. On Sunday he exercised for 14 minutes, although he only completed 10 of those minutes continuously. On Monday he exercised for 37 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Tuesday to reach his goal?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. On Tuesday, Tariq wants to exercise for eight times the amount of time he did on Monday and Sunday combined. On Sunday he exercised for 14 minutes, although he only completed 10 of those minutes continuously. On Monday he exercised for 37 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Tuesday to reach his goal?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate the total amount of time Tariq exercised on Monday and Sunday combined.\n2. Determine the target amount of time Tariq wants to exercise on Tuesday.\n3. Subtract the combined time from the target time to find out how much more time he needs to exercise on Tuesday.\n4. Ensure the final answer is in whole minutes.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's calculate step by step:\n1. Total time on Monday and Sunday combined:\n   Monday: 37 minutes\n   Sunday: 14 minutes\n   Combined: 37 + 14 = 51 minutes\n\n2. Target time for Tuesday:\n   Tariq wants to exercise for 8 times the combined time.\n   8 * 51 = 408 minutes\n\n3. Time needed on Tuesday:\n   Subtract the combined time from the target time.\n   408 - 51 = 357 minutes\n\nTherefore, Tariq needs to exercise for 357 minutes on Tuesday to reach his goal.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 357.0, "ground_truth": 408.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 695, "question": "On Friday, Viktor wants to exercise for thrice the amount of time he did on Thursday and Wednesday combined. On Wednesday he exercised for 58 minutes, although he took a 12-minute break halfway through. On Thursday he exercised for 51 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Friday to reach his goal?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. On Friday, Viktor wants to exercise for thrice the amount of time he did on Thursday and Wednesday combined. On Wednesday he exercised for 58 minutes, although he took a 12-minute break halfway through. On Thursday he exercised for 51 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Friday to reach his goal?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total time Viktor exercised on Wednesday and Thursday.\n2. Determine the total time he wants to exercise on Friday, which is three times the combined time from Wednesday and Thursday.\n3. Subtract the time he has already exercised on Wednesday and Thursday from the total time he wants to exercise on Friday to find out how much more time he needs to exercise on Friday.\n\nLet's start with step 1:\n- On Wednesday, Viktor exercised for 58 minutes, but he took a 12-minute break halfway through. Therefore, the actual exercise time on Wednesday is:\n  \\[\n  58 - 12 = 46 \\text{ minutes}\n  \\]\n- On Thursday, Viktor exercised for 51 minutes.\n\nNow, let's calculate the combined time for Wednesday and Thursday:\n\\[\n46 + 51 = 97 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nNext, we move to step 2:\n- Viktor wants to exercise for three times the combined time from Wednesday and Thursday on Friday. Therefore, the total time he wants to exercise on Friday is:\n  \\[\n  3 \\times 97 = 291 \\text{ minutes}\n  \\]\n\nFinally, we proceed to step 3:\n- To find out how much more time Viktor needs to exercise on Friday, we subtract the time he has already exercised on Wednesday and Thursday from the total time he wants to exercise on Friday:\n  \\[\n  291 - 97 = 194 \\text{ minutes}\n  \\]\n\nTherefore, Viktor needs to exercise for 194 more minutes on Friday to reach his goal.\n</think>\n<answer>\nViktor needs to exercise for 194 more minutes on Friday to reach his goal.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 194.0, "ground_truth": 327.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 696, "question": "On Friday, Luis wants to exercise for twice the amount of time he did on Thursday and Wednesday combined. On Wednesday he exercised for 13 minutes, even though he took 4 short breaks. On Thursday he exercised for 15 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Friday to reach his goal?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. On Friday, Luis wants to exercise for twice the amount of time he did on Thursday and Wednesday combined. On Wednesday he exercised for 13 minutes, even though he took 4 short breaks. On Thursday he exercised for 15 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Friday to reach his goal?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate the total time Luis exercised on Wednesday and Thursday combined.\n2. Determine the time Luis wants to exercise on Friday, which is twice the combined time from step 1.\n3. Subtract the combined time from step 1 from the time Luis wants to exercise on Friday to find out how much time he needs to exercise on Friday.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's calculate step by step:\n1. Total time exercised on Wednesday and Thursday combined:\n   Wednesday: 13 minutes\n   Thursday: 15 minutes\n   Combined: 13 + 15 = 28 minutes\n\n2. Time Luis wants to exercise on Friday:\n   Twice the combined time = 2 * 28 = 56 minutes\n\n3. Time Luis needs to exercise on Friday:\n   56 minutes (desired) - 28 minutes (combined) = 28 minutes\n\nTherefore, Luis needs to exercise for 28 minutes on Friday to reach his goal.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 28.0, "ground_truth": 56.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 697, "question": "On Saturday, Umar wants to exercise for ten times the amount of time he did on Friday and Thursday combined. On Thursday he exercised for 24 minutes, though he paused for 5 minutes to rest. On Friday he exercised for 16 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Saturday to reach his goal?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. On Saturday, Umar wants to exercise for ten times the amount of time he did on Friday and Thursday combined. On Thursday he exercised for 24 minutes, though he paused for 5 minutes to rest. On Friday he exercised for 16 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Saturday to reach his goal?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate the total time Umar exercised on Thursday and Friday combined.\n2. Determine the target time Umar wants to exercise on Saturday, which is ten times the combined time from step 1.\n3. Subtract the combined time from step 1 from the target time to find out how much more Umar needs to exercise on Saturday.\n4. Adjust for the 5-minute rest Umar took on Thursday.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, let's calculate the total time Umar exercised on Thursday and Friday combined:\n- On Thursday, Umar exercised for 24 minutes but paused for 5 minutes to rest. So, the actual exercise time on Thursday is 24 - 5 = 19 minutes.\n- On Friday, Umar exercised for 16 minutes.\n- Combined, the total exercise time on Thursday and Friday is 19 + 16 = 35 minutes.\n\nNext, we need to find out the target time Umar wants to exercise on Saturday:\n- The target time is ten times the combined time from step 1. So, 10 * 35 = 350 minutes.\n\nFinally, we need to determine how much more Umar needs to exercise on Saturday:\n- The difference between the target time and the combined time is 350 - 35 = 315 minutes.\n\nTherefore, Umar has to exercise for 315 minutes on Saturday to reach his goal.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 315.0, "ground_truth": 400.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 698, "question": "On Tuesday, Aisha wants to exercise for ten times the amount of time he did on Monday and Sunday combined. On Sunday he exercised for 41 minutes, even though 5 minutes were spent stretching. On Monday he exercised for 24 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Tuesday to reach his goal?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. On Tuesday, Aisha wants to exercise for ten times the amount of time he did on Monday and Sunday combined. On Sunday he exercised for 41 minutes, even though 5 minutes were spent stretching. On Monday he exercised for 24 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Tuesday to reach his goal?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate the total time Aisha exercised on Monday and Sunday combined.\n2. Determine the target time Aisha wants to exercise on Tuesday.\n3. Subtract the combined time from the target time to find out how much more time he needs to exercise on Tuesday.\n4. Ensure the final answer is in minutes.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's calculate step by step:\n1. Total time on Monday and Sunday combined:\n   - Monday: 24 minutes\n   - Sunday: 41 minutes (but we need to subtract the 5 minutes spent stretching)\n   Total = 24 + (41 - 5) = 24 + 36 = 60 minutes\n\n2. Target time for Tuesday:\n   - Aisha wants to exercise for ten times the combined time\n   Target = 10 * 60 = 600 minutes\n\n3. Time needed on Tuesday:\n   - We need to find out how much more time is required to reach the target\n   Time needed = Target - Combined time\n   Time needed = 600 - 60 = 540 minutes\n\nTherefore, Aisha needs to exercise for 540 minutes on Tuesday to reach his goal.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 540.0, "ground_truth": 650.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 699, "question": "On Monday, Qasim wants to exercise for twice the amount of time he did on Sunday and Saturday combined. On Saturday he exercised for 19 minutes, even though 5 of those minutes were at a slower pace. On Sunday he exercised for 44 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Monday to reach his goal?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. On Monday, Qasim wants to exercise for twice the amount of time he did on Sunday and Saturday combined. On Saturday he exercised for 19 minutes, even though 5 of those minutes were at a slower pace. On Sunday he exercised for 44 minutes. How many minutes does he have to exercise on Monday to reach his goal?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n1. Calculate the total exercise time Qasim did on Sunday and Saturday combined.\n2. Determine the target exercise time for Monday, which is twice the combined time from step 1.\n3. Subtract the combined time from step 1 from the target time for Monday to find out how many minutes he needs to exercise on Monday.\n4. Consider the 5 minutes of slower pace on Saturday as part of the total exercise time.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's calculate step by step:\n1. Total exercise time on Sunday and Saturday combined:\n   Sunday: 44 minutes\n   Saturday: 19 minutes (but 5 of those were slower pace, so we count the remaining 14 minutes)\n   Combined: 44 + 14 = 58 minutes\n\n2. Target exercise time for Monday:\n   Twice the combined time = 2 * 58 = 116 minutes\n\n3. Minutes Qasim needs to exercise on Monday:\n   116 (target) - 58 (combined Sunday and Saturday) = 58 minutes\n\nTherefore, Qasim needs to exercise for 58 minutes on Monday to reach his goal.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 58.0, "ground_truth": 126.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 700, "question": "A carpenter works for 3 weeks every month and for 4 days every week, but 2 of those days each week he spends on training instead of carpentry. If he gets paid ¥80 every day, how much does he earn if he works for a year?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A carpenter works for 3 weeks every month and for 4 days every week, but 2 of those days each week he spends on training instead of carpentry. If he gets paid ¥80 every day, how much does he earn if he works for a year?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine the total number of days the carpenter works in a month, then in a year. We also need to account for the days he spends on training. Finally, we can calculate his total earnings based on the daily wage.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's start by calculating the number of days the carpenter works in a month:\n1. He works 3 weeks every month.\n2. Each week he works 4 days, but 2 of those days are training days.\n3. Therefore, he works 4 - 2 = 2 days per week on carpentry.\n4. So, in a month, he works 3 weeks * 2 days/week = 6 days.\nNow, let's calculate the number of days he works in a year:\n1. There are 12 months in a year.\n2. He works 6 days per month.\n3. So, in a year, he works 12 months * 6 days/month = 72 days.\nNext, we need to calculate his total earnings:\n1. He gets paid ¥80 every day.\n2. So, his total earnings for the year would be 72 days * ¥80/day = ¥5760.\nTherefore, the carpenter earns ¥5760 in a year.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 5760.0, "ground_truth": 11520.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "GSM_Distracted", "item_id": 701, "question": "A carpenter works for 4 weeks every month and for 5 days every week, though he takes 3 days off each month for personal errands. If he gets paid ¥40 every day, how much does he earn if he works for a year?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A carpenter works for 4 weeks every month and for 5 days every week, though he takes 3 days off each month for personal errands. If he gets paid ¥40 every day, how much does he earn if he works for a year?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine the total number of days the carpenter works in a month. Then, we'll calculate the total number of days he works in a year. Finally, we'll multiply the total number of days worked by his daily wage to find his annual earnings.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's start by calculating the number of days the carpenter works in a month:\n1. He works 4 weeks every month.\n2. Each week he works 5 days.\n3. He takes 3 days off each month for personal errands.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of working days in a month:\n\\[ \\text{Working days per month} = 4 \\text{ weeks} \\times 5 \\text{ days/week} - 3 \\text{ days off} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Working days per month} = 20 - 3 = 17 \\text{ days} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the total number of working days in a year:\n\\[ \\text{Working days per year} = 17 \\text{ days/month} \\times 12 \\text{ months/year} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Working days per year} = 204 \\text{ days} \\]\n\nNow, we calculate his annual earnings:\n\\[ \\text{Annual earnings} = 204 \\text{ days} \\times ¥40/\\text{day} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Annual earnings} = ¥8160 \\]\n\nTherefore, the carpenter earns ¥8160 in a year.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 8160.0, "ground_truth": 9600.0, "is_correct": false}
