\text{Speed}_{\\text{sidewalk}} = 2 \\times \\frac{1}{4} \\text{ miles per minute} = \\frac{1}{2} \\text{ miles per minute} \\]\n\nNow, we need to calculate the time it takes her to walk the remaining 18 miles on the sidewalk. We can use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}} \\]\n\nSo, the time it takes her to walk 18 miles on the sidewalk is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time}_{\\text{sidewalk}} = \\frac{18 \\text{ miles}}{\\frac{1}{2} \\text{ miles per minute}} = 18 \\times 2 = 36 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nNow, we can add the time spent on the beach to the time spent on the sidewalk to get the total time for the entire 29-mile walk:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total time} = 44 \\text{ minutes} + 36 \\text{ minutes} = 80 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, it takes Kimia 80 minutes to complete the entire 29-mile walk.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 29.0, "ground_truth": 80.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "On the beach, Kimia walks at a rate of 11 miles per 44 minutes, or 11/44 = 1/4 miles per minute.\nOn the sidewalk, she walks at 2 times the rate of speed as when she is on the sand, or 2 * 1/4 = 1/2 miles per minute.\nTo walk 18 miles on the sidewalk, it takes her 18÷1/2=36 minutes.\nThus, in total, it takes Kimia 44+36=80 minutes to walk her favorite route.\n#### 80"}
