servings = 1425 ÷ 475\nNumber of servings = 3\nTherefore, you can eat 3 servings of cookies if your daily calorie target is 2350 and you have already consumed 925 calories.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 925.0, "ground_truth": 225.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "If the total calorie target is 2350 and I have consumed 925 calories then I have 2350-925 = 1425 calories left to eat\nIf each serving of cookies has 475 calories and I only have 1425 calories left to eat, then I can only eat 1425/475 of a serving = 3 of a serving\nWe also know that a 300 oz bag of cookies has 4 servings, hence each serving has 300 oz/4 = 75 oz\nIf I can only eat 3 of a serving, then I can eat only 75 * 3 = 75*3 = 225 oz\n#### 225"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 250, "question": "In Mrs. Garcia's class of 100 students, 20% of the class are swimmers. Out of the remaining class, 35% of the students are debate team members or part of dancers. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away meet. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mrs. Garcia's class of 100 students, 20% of the class are swimmers. Out of the remaining class, 35% of the students are debate team members or part of dancers. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away meet. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of students leaving early. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Calculate the number of swimmers:**\n   - Total students: 100\n   - Percentage of swimmers: 20%\n   - Number of swimmers = 20% of 100 = \\( \\frac{20}{100} \\times 100 = 20 \\)\n\n2. **Calculate the number of remaining students:**\n   - Total students: 100\n   - Number of swimmers: 20\n   - Remaining students = Total students - Number of swimmers = 100 - 20 = 80\n\n3. **Calculate the number of students in the debate team or dancers:**\n   - Percentage of remaining students in debate team or dancers: 35%\n   - Number of students in debate team or dancers = 35% of 80 = \\( \\frac{35}{100} \\times 80 = 28 \\)\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of students leaving early:**\n   - Number of swimmers: 20\n   - Number of students in debate team or dancers: 28\n   - Total students leaving early = Number of swimmers + Number of students in debate team or dancers = 20 + 28 = 48\n\nTherefore, the total number of students leaving early is 48.\n</think>\n<answer>48 students are leaving early.</answer>", "model_answer": 48.0, "ground_truth": 48.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "20% of the 100 student class are swimmers so that's 0.2*100 = 20 students\nThere are 100 students and 20 are swimmers so that leaves 100-20 = 80 students\n35% of the remaining 80 students are part of dancers or debate team members so that's 0.35*80 = 28 students\n20 students are swimmers and 28 are part of dancers/debate team members so 20+28 = 48 students will be leaving early\n#### 48"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 251, "question": "In Mr. Smith's class of 80 students, 25% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mr. Smith's class of 80 students, 25% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of students leaving early. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Calculate the number of basketball players:**\n   - Mr. Smith's class has 80 students.\n   - 25% of the class are basketball players.\n   - Number of basketball players = 25% of 80 = \\( \\frac{25}{100} \\times 80 = 20 \\) students.\n\n2. **Calculate the remaining students after accounting for basketball players:**\n   - Total students = 80\n   - Basketball players = 20\n   - Remaining students = Total students - Basketball players = 80 - 20 = 60 students.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of students who are dancers or part of the debate team:**\n   - Out of the remaining 60 students, 15% are dancers or part of the debate team.\n   - Number of dancers or debate team members = 15% of 60 = \\( \\frac{15}{100} \\times 60 = 9 \\) students.\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of students leaving early:**\n   - Basketball players leaving = 20\n   - Dancers or debate team members leaving = 9\n   - Total students leaving = Basketball players + Dancers or debate team members = 20 + 9 = 29 students.\n\nTherefore, the total number of students leaving early is 29.\n</think>\n<answer>29 students are leaving early.</answer>", "model_answer": 29.0, "ground_truth": 29.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "25% of the 80 student class are basketball players so that's 0.25*80 = 20 students\nThere are 80 students and 20 are basketball players so that leaves 80-20 = 60 students\n15% of the remaining 60 students are part of debate team members or dancers so that's 0.15*60 = 9 students\n20 students are basketball players and 9 are part of debate team members/dancers so 20+9 = 29 students will be leaving early\n#### 29"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 252, "question": "In Ms. Johnson's class of 130 students, 20% of the class are soccer players. Out of the remaining class, 25% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away competition. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Ms. Johnson's class of 130 students, 20% of the class are soccer players. Out of the remaining class, 25% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away competition. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of students leaving early. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Calculate the number of soccer players:**\n   - Ms. Johnson's class has 130 students.\n   - 20% of the class are soccer players.\n   - Number of soccer players = 20% of 130 = \\( 0.20 \\times 130 = 26 \\) students.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of students remaining after soccer players:**\n   - Total students = 130\n   - Soccer players = 26\n   - Remaining students = Total students - Soccer players = \\( 130 - 26 = 104 \\) students.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of students who are dancers or part of the debate team:**\n   - Out of the remaining 104 students, 25% are dancers or part of the debate team.\n   - Number of dancers or debate team members = 25% of 104 = \\( 0.25 \\times 104 = 26 \\) students.\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of students leaving early:**\n   - Soccer players = 26\n   - Dancers or debate team members = 26\n   - Total students leaving early = Soccer players + Dancers or debate team members = \\( 26 + 26 = 52 \\) students.\n\nTherefore, the total number of students leaving early is 52.\n</think>\n<answer>52 students are leaving early