{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 487, "question": "Beatriz can peel 10 clams a minute and saute 40 clams in 11 minutes. How long will it take her to peel and saute 80 clams?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Beatriz can peel 10 clams a minute and saute 40 clams in 11 minutes. How long will it take her to peel and saute 80 clams?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into two parts: peeling the clams and sautéing the clams. We'll calculate the time required for each part separately and then sum them up to get the total time.\n\n1. **Peeling the Clams:**\n   - Beatriz can peel 10 clams per minute.\n   - We need to peel 80 clams.\n   - Time to peel 80 clams = Total clams to peel / Clams peeled per minute = 80 clams / 10 clams/minute = 8 minutes.\n\n2. **Sautéing the Clams:**\n   - Beatriz can sauté 40 clams in 11 minutes.\n   - We need to sauté 80 clams.\n   - First, we need to determine how many 40-clam batches are in 80 clams.\n   - Number of 40-clam batches = Total clams to sauté / Clams sautéed per batch = 80 clams / 40 clams/batch = 2 batches.\n   - Since each batch takes 11 minutes, the total time for sautéing 80 clams = Number of batches * Time per batch = 2 batches * 11 minutes/batch = 22 minutes.\n\n3. **Total Time:**\n   - Total time = Time to peel 80 clams + Time to sauté 80 clams = 8 minutes + 22 minutes = 30 minutes.\n</think>\n<answer>\nIt will take Beatriz a total of 30 minutes to peel and sauté 80 clams.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 80.0, "ground_truth": 30.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "First find how long it takes Beatriz to peel the clam: 80 clam / 10 clam/minute = 8 minutes\nThen find how many batches of clam she needs to cook: 80 clam / 40 clam/batch = 2 batches\nThen multiply the number of batches by the time per batch to find the total cook time: 2 batches * 11 minutes/batch = 22 minutes\nThen add the peeling time to find the total time Beatriz spends: 22 minutes + 8 minutes = 30 minutes\n#### 30"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 488, "question": "Aisha can peel 12 clams a minute and saute 40 clams in 15 minutes. How long will it take her to peel and saute 120 clams?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Aisha can peel 12 clams a minute and saute 40 clams in 15 minutes. How long will it take her to peel and saute 120 clams?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into two parts: peeling the clams and sautéing the clams. We'll calculate the time required for each part separately and then sum them up to get the total time.\n\n1. **Peeling the Clams:**\n   - Aisha can peel 12 clams per minute.\n   - We need to find out how many minutes it takes to peel 120 clams.\n   - We use the formula: Time = Number of Clams / Rate.\n   - Plugging in the values: Time = 120 clams / 12 clams/minute = 10 minutes.\n\n2. **Sautéing the Clams:**\n   - Aisha can sauté 40 clams in 15 minutes.\n   - We need to find out how many minutes it takes to sauté 120 clams.\n   - We use the same formula: Time = Number of Clams / Rate.\n   - Plugging in the values: Time = 120 clams / 40 clams/15 minutes = 4.5 minutes.\n\n3. **Total Time:**\n   - We add the time taken to peel the clams and the time taken to sauté the clams.\n   - Total Time = Time to Peel + Time to Sauté.\n   - Total Time = 10 minutes + 4.5 minutes = 14.5 minutes.\n\nTherefore, it will take Aisha 14.5 minutes to peel and sauté 120 clams.\n</think>\n<answer>\n14.5 minutes\n</answer>", "model_answer": 14.5, "ground_truth": 55.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "First find how long it takes Aisha to peel the clam: 120 clam / 12 clam/minute = 10 minutes\nThen find how many batches of clam she needs to cook: 120 clam / 40 clam/batch = 3 batches\nThen multiply the number of batches by the time per batch to find the total cook time: 3 batches * 15 minutes/batch = 45 minutes\nThen add the peeling time to find the total time Aisha spends: 45 minutes + 10 minutes = 55 minutes\n#### 55"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 489, "question": "Amelia can peel 10 onions a minute and saute 25 onions in 9 minutes. How long will it take her to peel and saute 150 onions?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Amelia can peel 10 onions a minute and saute 25 onions in 9 minutes. How long will it take her to peel and saute 150 onions?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into two parts: peeling onions and sautéing onions. We know Amelia's rates for each task and need to determine the time required for each part and then combine them.\n1. Peeling onions: Amelia can peel 10 onions per minute. To find out how long it takes her to peel 150 onions, we divide the total number of onions by her peeling rate.\n2. Sautéing onions: Amelia can sauté 25 onions in 9 minutes. To find out how long it takes her to sauté 150 onions, we first determine the sauté rate and then use it to find the time needed.\nLet's calculate the time for each part:\n1. Peeling onions:\nTime = Total onions / Peeling rate\nTime = 150 onions / 10 onions per minute\nTime = 15 minutes\n2. Sautéing onions:\nFirst, we find the sauté rate:\nSauté rate = 25 onions / 9 minutes\nNow, we find the time needed to sauté 150 onions:\nTime = Total onions / Sauté rate\nTime = 150 onions / (25 onions / 9 minutes)\nTime = 150 onions * (9 minutes / 25 onions)\nTime = 54 minutes\nFinally, we add the time for peeling and sautéing:\nTotal time = Time for peeling + Time for sautéing\nTotal time = 15 minutes + 54 minutes\nTotal time = 69 minutes\n</think>\n<answer>\nAmelia will take 69 minutes to peel and sauté 150 onions.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 150.0, "ground_truth": 69.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "First find how long it takes Amelia to peel the onion: 150 onion / 10 onion/minute = 15 minutes\nThen find how many batches of onion she needs to cook: 150 onion / 25 onion/batch = 6 batches\nThen multiply the number of batches by the time per batch to find the total cook time: 6 batches * 9 minutes/batch = 54 minutes\nThen add the peeling time to find the total time Amelia spends: 54 minutes + 15 minutes = 69 minutes\n#### 69"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 490, "question": "Andre collects garbage from 7 different apartment complexes. The first 6 have 300 apartments each and the last one is 55% bigger than the other 6 combined. Andre collects garbage 2 times a week from each place and he gets paid €0.25 per collection for each apartment. How much money does he make in a week?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Andre collects garbage from 7 different apartment complexes. The first 6 have 300 apartments each and the last one is 55% bigger than the other 6 combined. Andre collects garbage 2 times a week from each place and he gets paid €0.25 per collection for each apartment. How much money does he make in a week?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of apartments in the first 6 complexes.\n2. Determine the number of apartments in the last complex.\n3. Find the total number of apartments across all complexes.\n4. Calculate the total number of garbage collections Andre makes in a week.\n5. Compute the total amount of money Andre earns in a week.\n\nLet's go through these steps one by one:\n\n1. **Total number of apartments in the first 6 complexes:**\n   Each of the first 6 complexes has 300 apartments.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total apartments in first 6 complexes} = 6 \\times 300 = 1800\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number of apartments in the last complex:**\n   The last complex has 55% more apartments than the total of the first 6 complexes.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Additional apartments} = 1800 \\times 0.55 = 990\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total apartments in the last complex} = 1800 + 990 = 2790\n   \\]\n\n3. **Total number of apartments across all complexes:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total apartments} = 1800 + 2790 = 4590\n   \\]\n\n4. **Total number of garbage collections in a week:**\n   Andre collects garbage 2 times a week from each apartment.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total collections per week} = 4590 \\times 2 = 9180\n   \\]\n\n5. **Total amount of money earned in a week:**\n   Andre earns €0.25 per collection.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total earnings} = 9180 \\times 0.25 = 2295\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, Andre makes €2295 in a week.\n</think>\n<answer>\n€2295\n</answer>", "model_answer": 2295.0, "ground_truth": 2295.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The first 6 complexes have 6*300=1800 apartments\nThe 7th one has 1800*0.55=990 more apartments than those 6 combined. So it has 1800+990=2790 apartments.\nSo in total, he goes to 2790+1800=4590 apartments each time\nThat means he visits 4590*2=9180 apartments every week\nSo he makes 9180*€0.25=€2295 every week\n#### 2295"}
