el a total of 90 miles in 10 hours if she spends one-half of the time running and two-fourths of the time walking.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 10.0, "ground_truth": 90.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "If Chen can skip at 1/3 the speed she can run, then she can run at 4*3=12 miles per hour.\nAnd since she can run at a speed that is 2 times faster than she can walk, this means she can walk at 12/2=6 miles per hour.\nIf 1/2 of the time is spent walking, then she walks for 10*1/2=5 hours.\nIf 1/2 of the time is spent running, then she runs for 10-5=5 hours.\nThus, she runs for 5 hours at 12 miles per hour, or 5*12=60 miles.\nShe walks for 5 hours at 6 miles per hour, or 5*6=30 miles.\nThus, altogether, she travels 60+30=90 miles.\n#### 90"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 888, "question": "Olivia can run nine times faster than she can walk, but she can skip at a rate of speed that is two-thirds as fast as she can run. If she can skip at 6 miles per hour, how many miles can she travel in 6 hours if she spends four-sixths of the time running and one-third of the time walking?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Olivia can run nine times faster than she can walk, but she can skip at a rate of speed that is two-thirds as fast as she can run. If she can skip at 6 miles per hour, how many miles can she travel in 6 hours if she spends four-sixths of the time running and one-third of the time walking?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Determine Olivia's running speed:**\n   - We know that Olivia can skip at 6 miles per hour, and this speed is two-thirds of her running speed.\n   - Let \\( R \\) be Olivia's running speed.\n   - According to the problem, \\( \\frac{2}{3}R = 6 \\) miles per hour.\n   - Solving for \\( R \\):\n     \\[\n     R = 6 \\times \\frac{3}{2} = 9 \\text{ miles per hour}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Determine the time spent running and walking:**\n   - The total time available is 6 hours.\n   - She spends four-sixths of the time running:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Time running} = \\frac{4}{6} \\times 6 = 4 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n   - She spends one-third of the time walking:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Time walking} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 6 = 2 \\text{ hours}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the distance covered while running and walking:**\n   - Distance covered while running:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Distance running} = \\text{Speed running} \\times \\text{Time running} = 9 \\text{ miles per hour} \\times 4 \\text{ hours} = 36 \\text{ miles}\n     \\]\n   - Distance covered while walking:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Distance walking} = \\text{Speed walking} \\times \\text{Time walking}\n     \\]\n     - We need to determine the walking speed. Since Olivia can run 9 miles per hour and walk at a rate that is nine times slower, her walking speed is:\n       \\[\n       \\text{Speed walking} = \\frac{9}{9} = 1 \\text{ mile per hour}\n       \\]\n     - Therefore, the distance walking is:\n       \\[\n       \\text{Distance walking} = 1 \\text{ mile per hour} \\times 2 \\text{ hours} = 2 \\text{ miles}\n       \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total distance covered:**\n   - Total distance covered is the sum of the distances covered while running and walking:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Total distance} = \\text{Distance running} + \\text{Distance walking} = 36 \\text{ miles} + 2 \\text{ miles} = 38 \\text{ miles}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the total distance Olivia can travel in 6 hours is 38 miles.\n</think>\n<answer>\nOlivia can travel a total of 38 miles in 6 hours if she spends four-sixths of the time running and one-third of the time walking.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 6.0, "ground_truth": 38.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "If Olivia can skip at 2/3 the speed she can run, then she can run at 6*3/2=9 miles per hour.\nAnd since she can run at a speed that is 9 times faster than she can walk, this means she can walk at 9/9=1 miles per hour.\nIf 1/3 of the time is spent walking, then she walks for 6*1/3=2 hours.\nIf 2/3 of the time is spent running, then she runs for 6-2=4 hours.\nThus, she runs for 4 hours at 9 miles per hour, or 4*9=36 miles.\nShe walks for 2 hours at 1 miles per hour, or 2*1=2 miles.\nThus, altogether, she travels 36+2=38 miles.\n#### 38"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 889, "question": "Winnie can run three times faster than she can walk, but she can skip at a rate of speed that is one-third as fast as she can run. If she can skip at 3 miles per hour, how many miles can she travel in 4 hours if she spends one-fourth of the time running and three-fourths of the time walking?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Winnie can run three times faster than she can walk, but she can skip at a rate of speed that is one-third as fast as she can run. If she can skip at 3 miles per hour, how many miles can she travel in 4 hours if she spends one-fourth of the time running and three-fourths of the time walking?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Determine Winnie's walking speed:**\n   - We know that Winnie can skip at 3 miles per hour.\n   - The skipping speed is one-third of her running speed.\n   - Therefore, her running speed is \\( 3 \\times 3 = 9 \\) miles per hour.\n   - Since she can run three times faster than she can walk, her walking speed is \\( \\frac{9}{3} = 3 \\) miles per hour.\n\n2. **Determine the time spent running and walking:**\n   - Winnie spends one-fourth of the time running and three-fourths of the time walking.\n   - The total time is 4 hours.\n   - Time spent running = \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\times 4 = 1 \\) hour.\n   - Time spent walking = \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\times 4 = 3 \\) hours.\n\n3. **Calculate the distance traveled while running and walking:**\n   - Distance traveled while running = Running speed × Time spent running = \\( 9 \\text{ miles/hour} \\times 1 \\text{ hour} = 9 \\text{ miles} \\).\n   - Distance traveled while walking = Walking speed × Time spent walking = \\( 3 \\text{ miles/hour} \\times 3 \\text{ hours} = 9 \\text{ miles} \\).\n\n4. **Calculate the total distance traveled:**\n   - Total distance = Distance traveled while running + Distance traveled while walking = \\( 9 \\text{ miles} + 9 \\text{ miles} = 18 \\text{ miles} \\).\n\nTherefore, Winnie can travel a total of 18 miles in 4 hours.\n</think>\n<answer>\nWinnie can travel a total of 18 miles in 4 hours.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 4.0, "ground_truth": 18.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "If Winnie can skip at 1/3 the speed she can run, then she can run at 3*3=9 miles per hour.\nAnd since she can run at a speed that is 3 times faster than she can walk, this means she can walk at 9/3=3 miles per hour.\nIf 3/4 of the time is spent walking, then she walks for 4*3/4=3 hours.\nIf 1/4 of the time is spent running, then she runs for 4-3=1 hours.\nThus, she runs for 1 hours at 9 miles per hour, or 1*9=9 miles.\nShe walks for 3 hours at 3 miles per hour, or 3*3=9 miles.\nThus, altogether, she travels 9+9=18 miles.\n#### 18"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 890, "question": "Ibrahim hires a limousine from 1 PM to 8 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is ₣46 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Ibrahim hires a limousine from 1 PM to 8 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is ₣46 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. First, we need to determine the total hours Ibrahim used the limousine. The limousine is hired from 1 PM to 8 PM, which is 7 hours in total.\n2. However, he gets 1 hour free. So, we need to subtract this 1 hour from the total hours used.\n3. This leaves us with 6 paid hours.\n4. The cost structure is as follows:\n   - The first hour is ₣46.\n   - Each hour after the first is thrice the cost of the previous hour.\n5. Let's calculate the cost for each of the 6 paid hours:\n   - 1st hour: ₣46\n   - 2nd hour: 3 * 46 = ₣138\n   - 3rd hour: 3 * 138 = ₣414\n   - 4th hour: 3 * 414 = ₣1242\n   - 5th hour: 3 * 1242 = ₣3726\n   - 6th hour: 3 * 3726 = ₣11178\n6. Now, we need to sum up the costs of these 6 hours to find the total amount Ibrahim paid.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe total amount Ibrahim paid is:\n46 + 138 + 414 + 1242 + 3726 + 11178 = ₣16706\nTherefore, Ibrahim paid ₣16706 for the limousine service.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 16706.0, "ground_truth": 736.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 8-1=7 hours\nHe pays for 7-1=6 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*46=₣46\nThe other 6-1=5 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 46*3=₣138 per hour\nSo those 5 hours cost 5*138=₣690\nSo he pays 690+46=₣736\n#### 736"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 891, "question": "Faisal hires a boat from 1 PM to 7 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is €25 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Faisal hires a boat from 1 PM to 7 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is €25 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. First, we need to determine the total time Faisal has the boat for. He hires it from 1 PM to 7 PM, which is 6 hours.\n2. However, he gets 1 hour of free use. So, we subtract this from the total time: 6 - 1 = 5 hours of paid use.\n3. The cost structure is as follows:\n   - The first hour is €25.\n   - Each hour after the first is three times the cost of the first hour.\n4. We need to calculate the cost for the remaining 4 hours (since the first hour is already accounted for in the €25).\n5. The cost for the second hour is 3 * €25 = €75.\n6. The cost for the third hour is also 3 * €25 = €75.\n7. The cost for the fourth hour is 3 * €25 = €75.\n8. Now, we add up the costs for the 4 paid hours: €25 + €75 + €75 + €75 = €250.\n9. Finally, we add the cost of the first hour to the total: €250 + €25 = €275.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFaisal paid a total of €275 for the boat rental.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 275.0, "ground_truth": 325.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 7-1=6 hours\nHe pays for 6-1=5 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*25=€25\nThe other 5-1=4 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 25*3=€75 per hour\nSo those 4 hours cost 4*75=€300\nSo he pays 300+25=€325\n#### 325"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 892, "question": "Wei hires a limousine from 4 PM to 10 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is €36 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Wei hires a limousine from 4 PM to 10 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is €36 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps:\n1. Determine the total hours Wei is using the limousine.\n2. Identify the free hours.\n3. Calculate the cost for the paid hours.\n4. Sum up the costs to find the total amount Wei paid.\nLet's go through these steps one by one:\n1. Wei hires the limousine from 4 PM to 10 PM, which is a total of 6 hours.\n2. He gets 1 hour free, so the paid hours are 6 - 1 = 5 hours.\n3. The cost structure is as follows:\n   - The first hour is €36.\n   - Each subsequent hour is twice the cost of the previous hour.\n   So, the costs for the paid hours are:\n   - 1st hour: €36\n   - 2nd hour: 2 * €36 = €72\n   - 3rd hour: 2 * €72 = €144\n   - 4th hour: 2 * €144 = €288\n   - 5th hour: 2 * €288 = €576\n4. Now, we sum up the costs for the paid hours:\n   €36 + €72 + €144 + €288 + €576 = €1116\nTherefore, Wei paid a total of €1116.\n</think>\n<answer>\nWei paid a total of €1116 for the limousine service.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 1116.0, "ground_truth": 324.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 10-4=6 hours\nHe pays for 6-1=5 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*36=€36\nThe other 5-1=4 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 36*2=€72 per hour\nSo those 4 hours cost 4*72=€288\nSo he pays 288+36=€324\n#### 324"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 893, "question": "Alejandro hires a boat from 4 PM to 9 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is ¥36 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Alejandro hires a boat from 4 PM to 9 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is ¥36 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. First, we need to determine the total time Alejandro has the boat. He hires it from 4 PM to 9 PM, which is 5 hours.\n2. However, he gets 1 hour of free use. So, we need to subtract this from the total time: 5 hours - 1 hour = 4 paid hours.\n3. The cost structure is as follows:\n   - The first hour is ¥36.\n   - Each subsequent hour is twice the cost of the previous hour.\n4. Let's calculate the cost for each of the 4 paid hours:\n   - First hour: ¥36\n   - Second hour: 2 * ¥36 = ¥72\n   - Third hour: 2 * ¥72 = ¥144\n   - Fourth hour: 2 * ¥144 = ¥288\n5. Now, we need to sum up the costs of these 4 hours to find the total amount Alejandro paid.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe total amount Alejandro paid is:\n¥36 + ¥72 + ¥144 + ¥288 = ¥540\nTherefore, Alejandro paid ¥540 for the boat rental.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 540.0, "ground_truth": 252.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 9-4=5 hours\nHe pays for 5-1=4 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*36=¥36\nThe other 4-1=3 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 36*2=¥72 per hour\nSo those 3 hours cost 3*72=¥216\nSo he pays 216+36=¥252\n#### 252"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 894, "question": "Arjun hires a luxury car from 4 PM to 10 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is ₣26 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Arjun hires a luxury car from 4 PM to 10 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is ₣26 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. First, we need to determine the total time Arjun had the car. He hired it from 4 PM to 10 PM, which is 6 hours.\n2. However, he gets 2 hours of free use. So, we need to subtract these 2 hours from the total time: 6 - 2 = 4 hours.\n3. Now, we need to calculate the cost for the 4 hours of paid use. The first hour costs ₣26, and each subsequent hour costs thrice the previous hour.\n4. Let's calculate the cost for each hour:\n   - First hour: ₣26\n   - Second hour: 3 * 26 = ₣78\n   - Third hour: 3 * 78 = ₣234\n   - Fourth hour: 3 * 234 = ₣702\n5. Now, we need to sum up the costs for the 4 paid hours: 26 + 78 + 234 + 702 = ₣1040\n</think>\n<answer>\nArjun paid a total of ₣1040 for the car rental.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 1040.0, "ground_truth": 260.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 10-4=6 hours\nHe pays for 6-2=4 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*26=₣26\nThe other 4-1=3 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 26*3=₣78 per hour\nSo those 3 hours cost 3*78=₣234\nSo he pays 234+26=₣260\n#### 260"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 895, "question": "Kai hires a luxury car from 1 PM to 5 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is $10 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Kai hires a luxury car from 1 PM to 5 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is $10 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. First, we need to determine the total time Kai has for the car rental. He hires it from 1 PM to 5 PM, which is 4 hours.\n2. We are told that he gets 2 hours of free use. So, we need to subtract these 2 hours from the total 4 hours.\n3. This leaves us with 2 hours that he needs to pay for.\n4. The cost structure is as follows:\n   - The first hour is $10.\n   - Each subsequent hour is twice the cost of the previous hour.\n5. Let's calculate the cost for the remaining 2 hours:\n   - First hour: $10\n   - Second hour: $10 * 2 = $20\n6. Now, we add up the costs for the 2 paid hours:\n   - Total cost = $10 + $20 = $30\n</think>\n<answer>\nKai paid a total of $30 for the car rental.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 30.0, "ground_truth": 30.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 5-1=4 hours\nHe pays for 4-2=2 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*10=$10\nThe other 2-1=1 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 10*2=$20 per hour\nSo those 1 hours cost 1*20=$20\nSo he pays 20+10=$30\n#### 30"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 896, "question": "Leon hires a luxury car from 3 PM to 9 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is ₺30 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Leon hires a luxury car from 3 PM to 9 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is ₺30 and each hour after that is thrice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps:\n1. Determine the total hours Leon has the car.\n2. Calculate the number of paid hours.\n3. Calculate the cost for each paid hour.\n4. Sum up the total cost.\nLet's go through these steps one by one:\n1. Leon hires the car from 3 PM to 9 PM, which is a total of 6 hours. However, he gets 1 hour free. Therefore, the number of paid hours is 6 - 1 = 5 hours.\n2. The first paid hour costs ₺30. The remaining 4 hours (5 paid hours - 1st hour) cost thrice the first hour. So, the cost for each of these 4 hours is 3 * ₺30 = ₺90.\n3. Now, we can calculate the total cost:\n   - Cost of the first hour: ₺30\n   - Cost of the next 4 hours: 4 * ₺90 = ₺360\n   - Total cost: ₺30 + ₺360 = ₺390\n</think>\n<answer>\nLeon paid ₺390 for the car rental.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 390.0, "ground_truth": 390.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 9-3=6 hours\nHe pays for 6-1=5 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*30=₺30\nThe other 5-1=4 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 30*3=₺90 per hour\nSo those 4 hours cost 4*90=₺360\nSo he pays 360+30=₺390\n#### 390"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 897, "question": "Oliver hires a party bus from 3 PM to 11 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is ¥31 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Oliver hires a party bus from 3 PM to 11 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is ¥31 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps:\n1. Determine the total time Oliver is using the bus.\n2. Calculate the number of paid hours.\n3. Calculate the cost for each paid hour.\n4. Sum up the total cost.\nLet's go through these steps one by one:\n1. Total time Oliver is using the bus: 11 PM - 3 PM = 8 hours.\n2. Subtract the 2 hours of free time: 8 hours - 2 hours = 6 paid hours.\n3. The cost structure is ¥31 for the first hour and double for each subsequent hour. So, the cost for the first hour is ¥31, and for the next 5 hours, it will be ¥62 each.\n4. Now, let's calculate the total cost:\n   - First hour: ¥31\n   - Next 5 hours: 5 * ¥62 = ¥310\n   - Total cost: ¥31 + ¥310 = ¥341\n</think>\n<answer>\nOliver paid a total of ¥341 for the party bus service.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 341.0, "ground_truth": 341.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 11-3=8 hours\nHe pays for 8-2=6 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*31=¥31\nThe other 6-1=5 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 31*2=¥62 per hour\nSo those 5 hours cost 5*62=¥310\nSo he pays 310+31=¥341\n#### 341"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 898, "question": "Qasim hires a limousine from 2 PM to 11 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is ₣40 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Qasim hires a limousine from 2 PM to 11 PM. He gets 2 hours free. The first paid hour is ₣40 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. First, we need to determine the total time Qasim hired the limousine. He hired it from 2 PM to 11 PM, which is 9 hours.\n2. However, he gets 2 hours free. So, we need to subtract these 2 hours from the total time:\n   9 hours - 2 hours = 7 hours\n3. Now, we need to calculate the cost for the 7 hours. The first hour costs ₣40, and each subsequent hour costs twice the previous hour.\n4. Let's calculate the cost for each hour:\n   - 1st hour: ₣40\n   - 2nd hour: 2 * ₣40 = ₣80\n   - 3rd hour: 2 * ₣80 = ₣160\n   - 4th hour: 2 * ₣160 = ₣320\n   - 5th hour: 2 * ₣320 = ₣640\n   - 6th hour: 2 * ₣640 = ₣1280\n   - 7th hour: 2 * ₣1280 = ₣2560\n5. Now, we need to sum up the costs for these 7 hours:\n   Total cost = ₣40 + ₣80 + ₣160 + ₣320 + ₣640 + ₣1280 + ₣2560\n   Total cost = ₣5080\n</think>\n<answer>\nQasim paid a total of ₣5080 for the limousine hire.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 5080.0, "ground_truth": 520.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 11-2=9 hours\nHe pays for 9-2=7 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*40=₣40\nThe other 7-1=6 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 40*2=₣80 per hour\nSo those 6 hours cost 6*80=₣480\nSo he pays 480+40=₣520\n#### 520"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 899, "question": "Andre hires a limousine from 5 PM to 11 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is $24 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Andre hires a limousine from 5 PM to 11 PM. He gets 1 hours free. The first paid hour is $24 and each hour after that is twice the cost. How much did he pay?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. First, we need to determine the total hours Andre was supposed to pay for. The limousine was hired from 5 PM to 11 PM, which is a total of 6 hours.\n2. However, Andre gets 1 hour free. So, we need to subtract this 1 hour from the total hours:\n   6 hours - 1 hour = 5 hours\n3. Now, we need to calculate the cost for these 5 hours. The first hour is $24, and each subsequent hour is twice the cost of the previous hour.\n4. Let's calculate the cost for each hour:\n   - 1st hour: $24\n   - 2nd hour: $24 * 2 = $48\n   - 3rd hour: $48 * 2 = $96\n   - 4th hour: $96 * 2 = $192\n   - 5th hour: $192 * 2 = $384\n5. Now, we need to sum up the costs for these 5 hours:\n   $24 + $48 + $96 + $192 + $384 = $840\n</think>\n<answer>\nAndre paid a total of $840 for the limousine service.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 840.0, "ground_truth": 216.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "He got it for 11-5=6 hours\nHe pays for 6-1=5 hours\nThe first hour cost 1*24=$24\nThe other 5-1=4 hours are more expensive\nThey cost 24*2=$48 per hour\nSo those 4 hours cost 4*48=$192\nSo he pays 192+24=$216\n#### 216"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 900, "question": "Winnie has three times as many Twitter friends as Amelia. Valeria has twice as many friends on Twitter as Amelia. If Winnie has 12 friends on Twitter, how many Twitter friends does Valeria have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Winnie has three times as many Twitter friends as Amelia. Valeria has twice as many friends on Twitter as Amelia. If Winnie has 12 friends on Twitter, how many Twitter friends does Valeria have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n1. We know that Winnie has three times as many Twitter friends as Amelia.\n2. We also know that Valeria has twice as many friends on Twitter as Amelia.\n3. We are given that Winnie has 12 Twitter friends.\n4. Let's denote the number of Amelia's Twitter friends as A.\n5. According to the first piece of information, Winnie's friends = 3 * Amelia's friends, which translates to 12 = 3A.\n6. We can solve this equation to find A:\n   12 = 3A\n   A = 12 / 3\n   A = 4\n7. Now that we know Amelia has 4 Twitter friends, we can find out how many Valeria has:\n   Valeria's friends = 2 * Amelia's friends\n   Valeria's friends = 2 * 4\n   Valeria's friends = 8\n</think>\n<answer>\nValeria has 8 Twitter friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 8.0, "ground_truth": 8.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Amelia has 12 / 3 = 4 Twitter friends.\nValeria has 2 * 4 = 8 Twitter friends.\n#### 8"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 901, "question": "Deshawn has twice as many Snapchat friends as Elena. Sophia has thrice as many friends on Snapchat as Elena. If DeShawn has 168 friends on Snapchat, how many Snapchat friends does Sophia have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Deshawn has twice as many Snapchat friends as Elena. Sophia has thrice as many friends on Snapchat as Elena. If DeShawn has 168 friends on Snapchat, how many Snapchat friends does Sophia have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to establish relationships between the number of friends each person has on Snapchat. We are given that Deshawn has twice as many friends as Elena and that Sophia has thrice as many friends as Elena. We are also given that Deshawn has 168 friends. We need to find out how many friends Sophia has.\nFirst, let's denote the number of friends Elena has as \\( E \\). According to the problem, Deshawn has twice as many friends as Elena, so we can write:\n\\[ \\text{Deshawn's friends} = 2E \\]\nWe are given that Deshawn has 168 friends, so:\n\\[ 2E = 168 \\]\nTo find \\( E \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 2:\n\\[ E = \\frac{168}{2} = 84 \\]\nNow that we know Elena has 84 friends, we can find out how many friends Sophia has. According to the problem, Sophia has thrice as many friends as Elena, so we can write:\n\\[ \\text{Sophia's friends} = 3E \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( E \\) we found:\n\\[ \\text{Sophia's friends} = 3 \\times 84 = 252 \\]\nTherefore, Sophia has 252 friends on Snapchat.\n</think>\n<answer>\nSophia has 252 Snapchat friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 252.0, "ground_truth": 252.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Elena has 168 / 2 = 84 Snapchat friends.\nSophia has 3 * 84 = 252 Snapchat friends.\n#### 252"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 902, "question": "Yara has seven times as many LinkedIn friends as Luis. Jamal has two times as many friends on LinkedIn as Luis. If Yara has 91 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Jamal have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Yara has seven times as many LinkedIn friends as Luis. Jamal has two times as many friends on LinkedIn as Luis. If Yara has 91 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Jamal have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to establish the relationships between the number of friends each person has on LinkedIn. We are given that Yara has seven times as many friends as Luis, and Jamal has two times as many friends as Luis. We are also given that Yara has 91 friends. We need to find out how many friends Jamal has.\nFirst, let's denote the number of friends Luis has as \\( L \\). According to the problem, Yara has seven times as many friends as Luis, so we can write:\n\\[ Y = 7L \\]\nWe are given that Yara has 91 friends, so:\n\\[ 7L = 91 \\]\nTo find \\( L \\), we can solve this equation by dividing both sides by 7:\n\\[ L = \\frac{91}{7} = 13 \\]\nNow that we know Luis has 13 friends, we can find out how many friends Jamal has. According to the problem, Jamal has two times as many friends as Luis, so:\n\\[ J = 2L \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( L \\) we found:\n\\[ J = 2 \\times 13 = 26 \\]\nTherefore, Jamal has 26 friends on LinkedIn.\n</think>\n<answer>\nJamal has 26 LinkedIn friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 26.0, "ground_truth": 26.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Luis has 91 / 7 = 13 LinkedIn friends.\nJamal has 2 * 13 = 26 LinkedIn friends.\n#### 26"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 903, "question": "Santiago has four times as many Snapchat friends as Priya. Gabrielle has seven times as many friends on Snapchat as Priya. If Santiago has 108 friends on Snapchat, how many Snapchat friends does Gabrielle have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Santiago has four times as many Snapchat friends as Priya. Gabrielle has seven times as many friends on Snapchat as Priya. If Santiago has 108 friends on Snapchat, how many Snapchat friends does Gabrielle have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine the number of Snapchat friends Priya has first, and then use that information to find out how many friends Gabrielle has. We know that Santiago has four times as many friends as Priya, and Santiago has 108 friends. We can set up an equation to find the number of friends Priya has.\nLet's denote the number of friends Priya has as \\( P \\). According to the problem, Santiago has four times as many friends as Priya, so we can write:\n\\[ 4P = 108 \\]\nTo find \\( P \\), we need to solve this equation.\n</think>\n<answer>\nDividing both sides of the equation by 4, we get:\n\\[ P = \\frac{108}{4} = 27 \\]\nSo, Priya has 27 friends on Snapchat.\nNow, we know that Gabrielle has seven times as many friends as Priya. Let's denote the number of friends Gabrielle has as \\( G \\). We can write:\n\\[ G = 7P \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( P \\) we found:\n\\[ G = 7 \\times 27 \\]\nNow, we need to calculate \\( 7 \\times 27 \\).\n</answer>", "model_answer": 27.0, "ground_truth": 189.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Priya has 108 / 4 = 27 Snapchat friends.\nGabrielle has 7 * 27 = 189 Snapchat friends.\n#### 189"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 904, "question": "Zahra has three times as many Twitter friends as Santiago. Alejandro has two times as many friends on Twitter as Santiago. If Zahra has 78 friends on Twitter, how many Twitter friends does Alejandro have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Zahra has three times as many Twitter friends as Santiago. Alejandro has two times as many friends on Twitter as Santiago. If Zahra has 78 friends on Twitter, how many Twitter friends does Alejandro have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to establish the relationships between the number of friends each person has on Twitter and use the given information to find the number of friends Alejandro has. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - Zahra has three times as many Twitter friends as Santiago.\n   - Alejandro has two times as many Twitter friends as Santiago.\n   - Zahra has 78 Twitter friends.\n\n2. **Define variables:**\n   - Let \\( S \\) represent the number of Twitter friends Santiago has.\n   - Zahra has three times as many friends as Santiago, so Zahra's friends can be represented as \\( 3S \\).\n   - We know Zahra has 78 friends, so we can set up the equation:\n     \\[\n     3S = 78\n     \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\( S \\):**\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{78}{3} = 26\n   \\]\n   Therefore, Santiago has 26 Twitter friends.\n\n4. **Determine the number of friends Alejandro has:**\n   - Alejandro has two times as many friends as Santiago, so Alejandro's friends can be represented as \\( 2S \\).\n   - Since \\( S = 26 \\), we substitute this value into the equation:\n     \\[\n     2S = 2 \\times 26 = 52\n     \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - Alejandro has 52 Twitter friends.\n</think>\n<answer>\nAlejandro has 52 Twitter friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 52.0, "ground_truth": 52.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Santiago has 78 / 3 = 26 Twitter friends.\nAlejandro has 2 * 26 = 52 Twitter friends.\n#### 52"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 905, "question": "Theo has five times as many LinkedIn friends as Nadia. Sanjay has twice as many friends on LinkedIn as Nadia. If Theo has 190 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Sanjay have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Theo has five times as many LinkedIn friends as Nadia. Sanjay has twice as many friends on LinkedIn as Nadia. If Theo has 190 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Sanjay have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to establish the relationships between the number of friends each person has on LinkedIn. We are given that Theo has five times as many friends as Nadia and that Sanjay has twice as many friends as Nadia. We are also given that Theo has 190 friends. We need to find out how many friends Sanjay has.\nFirst, let's denote the number of friends Nadia has as \\( N \\). According to the problem, Theo has five times as many friends as Nadia, so we can write:\n\\[ 5N = 190 \\]\nTo find \\( N \\), we can solve this equation by dividing both sides by 5:\n\\[ N = \\frac{190}{5} = 38 \\]\nSo, Nadia has 38 friends.\nNext, we need to find out how many friends Sanjay has. The problem states that Sanjay has twice as many friends as Nadia. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[ \\text{Number of friends Sanjay has} = 2N \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( N \\) we found:\n\\[ \\text{Number of friends Sanjay has} = 2 \\times 38 = 76 \\]\nThus, Sanjay has 76 friends.\n</think>\n<answer>\nSanjay has 76 LinkedIn friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 76.0, "ground_truth": 76.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Nadia has 190 / 5 = 38 LinkedIn friends.\nSanjay has 2 * 38 = 76 LinkedIn friends.\n#### 76"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 906, "question": "Beatriz has four times as many Snapchat friends as Liam. Leilani has eight times as many friends on Snapchat as Liam. If Beatriz has 48 friends on Snapchat, how many Snapchat friends does Leilani have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Beatriz has four times as many Snapchat friends as Liam. Leilani has eight times as many friends on Snapchat as Liam. If Beatriz has 48 friends on Snapchat, how many Snapchat friends does Leilani have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to establish the relationships between the number of friends each person has on Snapchat and use the given information to find the number of Leilani's friends. Let's denote the number of Liam's friends on Snapchat as \\( L \\).\n\nFrom the problem, we know:\n1. Beatriz has four times as many friends as Liam.\n2. Leilani has eight times as many friends as Liam.\n3. Beatriz has 48 friends on Snapchat.\n\nWe can set up the following equations based on the given information:\n- Beatriz's friends = \\( 4L \\)\n- Leilani's friends = \\( 8L \\)\n\nGiven that Beatriz has 48 friends:\n\\[ 4L = 48 \\]\n\nWe can solve for \\( L \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 4:\n\\[ L = \\frac{48}{4} = 12 \\]\n\nNow that we know Liam has 12 friends on Snapchat, we can find the number of Leilani's friends:\n\\[ \\text{Leilani's friends} = 8L = 8 \\times 12 = 96 \\]\n\nTherefore, Leilani has 96 friends on Snapchat.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLeilani has 96 Snapchat friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 96.0, "ground_truth": 96.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Liam has 48 / 4 = 12 Snapchat friends.\nLeilani has 8 * 12 = 96 Snapchat friends.\n#### 96"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 907, "question": "Bodhi has nine times as many Twitter friends as Viktor. Chen has seven times as many friends on Twitter as Viktor. If Bodhi has 27 friends on Twitter, how many Twitter friends does Chen have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Bodhi has nine times as many Twitter friends as Viktor. Chen has seven times as many friends on Twitter as Viktor. If Bodhi has 27 friends on Twitter, how many Twitter friends does Chen have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of Twitter friends Chen has. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - Bodhi has 27 Twitter friends.\n   - Bodhi has nine times as many Twitter friends as Viktor.\n   - Chen has seven times as many Twitter friends as Viktor.\n\n2. **Determine the number of Viktor's Twitter friends:**\n   - Since Bodhi has nine times as many friends as Viktor, we can set up the equation:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Bodhi's friends} = 9 \\times \\text{Viktor's friends}\n     \\]\n   - Given that Bodhi has 27 friends:\n     \\[\n     27 = 9 \\times \\text{Viktor's friends}\n     \\]\n   - To find Viktor's friends, we divide both sides by 9:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Viktor's friends} = \\frac{27}{9} = 3\n     \\]\n\n3. **Determine the number of Chen's Twitter friends:**\n   - Now that we know Viktor has 3 Twitter friends, we can find Chen's friends using the given ratio:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Chen's friends} = 7 \\times \\text{Viktor's friends}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting Viktor's friends:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Chen's friends} = 7 \\times 3 = 21\n     \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - Chen has 21 Twitter friends.\n</think>\n<answer>\nChen has 21 Twitter friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 21.0, "ground_truth": 21.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Viktor has 27 / 9 = 3 Twitter friends.\nChen has 7 * 3 = 21 Twitter friends.\n#### 21"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 908, "question": "Amelia has three times as many LinkedIn friends as Zahra. Mei has ten times as many friends on LinkedIn as Zahra. If Amelia has 45 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Mei have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Amelia has three times as many LinkedIn friends as Zahra. Mei has ten times as many friends on LinkedIn as Zahra. If Amelia has 45 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Mei have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to establish the relationships between the number of friends each person has on LinkedIn. We are given that Amelia has three times as many friends as Zahra and that Mei has ten times as many friends as Zahra. We are also given that Amelia has 45 friends. We need to find out how many friends Mei has.\nFirst, let's denote the number of friends Zahra has as \\( Z \\). According to the problem, Amelia has three times as many friends as Zahra, so we can write:\n\\[ \\text{Amelia's friends} = 3Z \\]\nWe are given that Amelia has 45 friends, so:\n\\[ 3Z = 45 \\]\nTo find \\( Z \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 3:\n\\[ Z = \\frac{45}{3} = 15 \\]\nNow that we know Zahra has 15 friends, we can find out how many friends Mei has. Mei has ten times as many friends as Zahra, so:\n\\[ \\text{Mei's friends} = 10Z \\]\nSubstituting \\( Z = 15 \\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Mei's friends} = 10 \\times 15 = 150 \\]\nTherefore, Mei has 150 friends on LinkedIn.\n</think>\n<answer>\nMei has 150 LinkedIn friends.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 150.0, "ground_truth": 150.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Zahra has 45 / 3 = 15 LinkedIn friends.\nMei has 10 * 15 = 150 LinkedIn friends.\n#### 150"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 909, "question": "Kimia has thrice as many LinkedIn friends as Aisha. Mateo has four times as many friends on LinkedIn as Aisha. If Kimia has 156 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Mateo have?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Kimia has thrice as many LinkedIn friends as Aisha. Mateo has four times as many friends on LinkedIn as Aisha. If Kimia has 156 friends on LinkedIn, how many LinkedIn friends does Mateo have?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to establish the relationships between the number of friends each person has on LinkedIn. We are given that Kimia has thrice as many friends as Aisha and that Kimia has 156 friends. We can use this information to find out how many friends Aisha has. Then, we can use the fact that Mateo has four times as many friends as Aisha to find out how many friends Mateo has.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the number of friends Aisha has as \\( A \\). According to the problem, Kimia has thrice as many friends as Aisha, which can be written as:\n\\[ 3A = 156 \\]\n\nTo find \\( A \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 3:\n\\[ A = \\frac{156}{3} = 52 \\]\n\nSo, Aisha has 52 friends on LinkedIn.\n\nNext, we need to find out how many friends Mateo has. The problem states that Mateo has four times as many friends as Aisha. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[ \\text{Number of friends Mateo has} = 4A \\]\n\nSubstituting the value of \\( A \\) we found:\n\\[ \\text{Number of friends Mateo has} = 4 \\times 52 = 208 \\]\n\nThus, Mateo has 208 friends on LinkedIn.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 208.0, "ground_truth": 208.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Aisha has 156 / 3 = 52 LinkedIn friends.\nMateo has 4 * 52 = 208 LinkedIn friends.\n#### 208"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 910, "question": "At Wei's house, there are ten times as many plums as bananas. He has a total of 38 bananas in his house. Wei bought 46 more bananas at the store and 45 fewer plums than the number of bananas. Find the combined total of the number of plums and bananas Wei has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Wei's house, there are ten times as many plums as bananas. He has a total of 38 bananas in his house. Wei bought 46 more bananas at the store and 45 fewer plums than the number of bananas. Find the combined total of the number of plums and bananas Wei has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of plums and bananas Wei has in his house. We'll start by defining the variables and using the given information to form equations.\n1. Let's denote the number of bananas Wei has initially as \\( B \\).\n2. According to the problem, Wei has 38 bananas initially, so \\( B = 38 \\).\n3. Wei has ten times as many plums as bananas, so the number of plums \\( P \\) can be expressed as \\( P = 10B \\).\n4. Wei bought 46 more bananas at the store, so the new number of bananas \\( B_{new} \\) is \\( B_{new} = B + 46 \\).\n5. Wei bought 45 fewer plums than the number of bananas, so the new number of plums \\( P_{new} \\) is \\( P_{new} = P - 45 \\).\n6. We need to find the combined total of the number of plums and bananas Wei has in the house, which is \\( B_{new} + P_{new} \\).\n</think>\n<answer>\nNow, let's substitute the values and calculate the combined total.\n1. Initial number of bananas \\( B = 38 \\).\n2. Number of plums initially \\( P = 10B = 10 \\times 38 = 380 \\).\n3. New number of bananas after buying more \\( B_{new} = B + 46 = 38 + 46 = 84 \\).\n4. New number of plums after buying fewer \\( P_{new} = P - 45 = 380 - 45 = 335 \\).\n5. Combined total of plums and bananas \\( B_{new} + P_{new} = 84 + 335 = 419 \\).\n\nTherefore, the combined total of the number of plums and bananas Wei has in the house is 419.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 419.0, "ground_truth": 465.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any plum and banana, Wei had 10 times as many plums as bananas, which is 38 bananas * 10 plum/banana = 380 plums\nThe total number of plums and bananas that Wei had before is 380 plums + 38 bananas = 418 items\nWhen he bought 45 fewer plums than bananas, he bought 46 plums - 45 plums = 1 plums\nIn total, he bought 1 plums + 46 bananas = 47 items\nAfter the purchases, Wei has 418 items + 47 items = 465 total plums and bananas combined.\n#### 465"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 911, "question": "At Jamal's house, there are five times as many bananas as pears. He has a total of 49 pears in his house. Jamal bought 70 more pears at the store and 39 fewer bananas than the number of pears. Find the combined total of the number of bananas and pears Jamal has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Jamal's house, there are five times as many bananas as pears. He has a total of 49 pears in his house. Jamal bought 70 more pears at the store and 39 fewer bananas than the number of pears. Find the combined total of the number of bananas and pears Jamal has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine the initial number of bananas and pears Jamal has. We know that there are five times as many bananas as pears, and Jamal has 49 pears. We can use this information to find the initial number of bananas. Then, we need to account for the additional pears Jamal bought and the change in the number of bananas. Finally, we will sum the total number of bananas and pears to get the final answer.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's start by finding the initial number of bananas. Since there are five times as many bananas as pears, and Jamal has 49 pears, we can calculate the initial number of bananas as follows:\nInitial number of bananas = 5 * Initial number of pears\nInitial number of bananas = 5 * 49\nInitial number of bananas = 245\n\nNext, we need to account for the additional pears Jamal bought. He bought 70 more pears, so we add this to the initial number of pears:\nTotal number of pears = Initial number of pears + Additional pears\nTotal number of pears = 49 + 70\nTotal number of pears = 119\n\nNow, we need to find the number of bananas Jamal has after buying the additional pears. We know that he bought 39 fewer bananas than the number of pears he had initially. So, we subtract 39 from the initial number of bananas:\nNumber of bananas after buying pears = Initial number of bananas - 39\nNumber of bananas after buying pears = 245 - 39\nNumber of bananas after buying pears = 206\n\nFinally, we can find the combined total of the number of bananas and pears Jamal has in the house by adding the total number of pears and the number of bananas after buying pears:\nCombined total = Total number of pears + Number of bananas after buying pears\nCombined total = 119 + 206\nCombined total = 325\n\nTherefore, the combined total of the number of bananas and pears Jamal has in the house is 325.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 325.0, "ground_truth": 395.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any banana and pear, Jamal had 5 times as many bananas as pears, which is 49 pears * 5 banana/pear = 245 bananas\nThe total number of bananas and pears that Jamal had before is 245 bananas + 49 pears = 294 items\nWhen he bought 39 fewer bananas than pears, he bought 70 bananas - 39 bananas = 31 bananas\nIn total, he bought 31 bananas + 70 pears = 101 items\nAfter the purchases, Jamal has 294 items + 101 items = 395 total bananas and pears combined.\n#### 395"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 912, "question": "At Yuki's house, there are five times as many plums as figs. He has a total of 63 figs in his house. Yuki bought 67 more figs at the store and 47 fewer plums than the number of figs. Find the combined total of the number of plums and figs Yuki has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Yuki's house, there are five times as many plums as figs. He has a total of 63 figs in his house. Yuki bought 67 more figs at the store and 47 fewer plums than the number of figs. Find the combined total of the number of plums and figs Yuki has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of plums and figs Yuki has in his house. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Initial Number of Figs:**\n   - We are given that Yuki initially has 63 figs.\n\n2. **Number of Plums:**\n   - The problem states that there are five times as many plums as figs.\n   - Therefore, the number of plums can be calculated as:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Number of plums} = 5 \\times \\text{Number of figs} = 5 \\times 63\n     \\]\n   - Calculating this:\n     \\[\n     5 \\times 63 = 315\n     \\]\n   - So, Yuki initially has 315 plums.\n\n3. **After Buying More Figs:**\n   - Yuki bought 67 more figs at the store.\n   - The new number of figs is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{New number of figs} = 63 + 67\n     \\]\n   - Calculating this:\n     \\[\n     63 + 67 = 130\n     \\]\n   - So, Yuki now has 130 figs.\n\n4. **Number of Plums After Buying Fewer Plums:**\n   - Yuki bought 47 fewer plums than the number of figs he had after buying more figs.\n   - The number of plums he bought is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Number of plums bought} = 130 - 47\n     \\]\n   - Calculating this:\n     \\[\n     130 - 47 = 83\n     \\]\n   - So, Yuki now has:\n     \\[\n     \\text{New number of plums} = 315 - 83\n     \\]\n   - Calculating this:\n     \\[\n     315 - 83 = 232\n     \\]\n   - So, Yuki now has 232 plums.\n\n5. **Combined Total of Plums and Figs:**\n   - To find the combined total, we add the new number of plums and the new number of figs:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Combined total} = \\text{New number of plums} + \\text{New number of figs}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting the values:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Combined total} = 232 + 130\n     \\]\n   - Calculating this:\n     \\[\n     232 + 130 = 362\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the combined total of the number of plums and figs Yuki has in his house is 362.\n</think>\n<answer>362</answer>", "model_answer": 362.0, "ground_truth": 465.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any plum and fig, Yuki had 5 times as many plums as figs, which is 63 figs * 5 plum/fig = 315 plums\nThe total number of plums and figs that Yuki had before is 315 plums + 63 figs = 378 items\nWhen he bought 47 fewer plums than figs, he bought 67 plums - 47 plums = 20 plums\nIn total, he bought 20 plums + 67 figs = 87 items\nAfter the purchases, Yuki has 378 items + 87 items = 465 total plums and figs combined.\n#### 465"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 913, "question": "At Gabriel's house, there are five times as many grapes as apples. He has a total of 94 apples in his house. Gabriel bought 42 more apples at the store and 39 fewer grapes than the number of apples. Find the combined total of the number of grapes and apples Gabriel has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Gabriel's house, there are five times as many grapes as apples. He has a total of 94 apples in his house. Gabriel bought 42 more apples at the store and 39 fewer grapes than the number of apples. Find the combined total of the number of grapes and apples Gabriel has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of grapes and apples Gabriel has, and then find the combined total. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Determine the initial number of apples:**\n   - Gabriel initially has 94 apples.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of grapes:**\n   - According to the problem, there are five times as many grapes as apples.\n   - Therefore, the number of grapes = 5 * number of apples.\n   - Substituting the initial number of apples: \n     \\[\n     \\text{Number of grapes} = 5 \\times 94 = 470\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the new number of apples after buying more:**\n   - Gabriel bought 42 more apples.\n   - Therefore, the new number of apples = initial number of apples + additional apples.\n   - Substituting the values:\n     \\[\n     \\text{New number of apples} = 94 + 42 = 136\n     \\]\n\n4. **Determine the new number of grapes:**\n   - The problem states that Gabriel bought 39 fewer grapes than the number of apples.\n   - Therefore, the new number of grapes = new number of apples - 39.\n   - Substituting the new number of apples:\n     \\[\n     \\text{New number of grapes} = 136 - 39 = 97\n     \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the combined total of grapes and apples:**\n   - The combined total = new number of grapes + new number of apples.\n   - Substituting the values:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Combined total} = 97 + 136 = 233\n     \\]\n\nThus, the combined total of the number of grapes and apples Gabriel has in the house is 233.\n</think>\n<answer>233</answer>", "model_answer": 233.0, "ground_truth": 609.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any grape and apple, Gabriel had 5 times as many grapes as apples, which is 94 apples * 5 grape/apple = 470 grapes\nThe total number of grapes and apples that Gabriel had before is 470 grapes + 94 apples = 564 items\nWhen he bought 39 fewer grapes than apples, he bought 42 grapes - 39 grapes = 3 grapes\nIn total, he bought 3 grapes + 42 apples = 45 items\nAfter the purchases, Gabriel has 564 items + 45 items = 609 total grapes and apples combined.\n#### 609"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 914, "question": "At Tariq's house, there are five times as many corns as pears. He has a total of 48 pears in his house. Tariq bought 55 more pears at the store and 34 fewer corns than the number of pears. Find the combined total of the number of corns and pears Tariq has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Tariq's house, there are five times as many corns as pears. He has a total of 48 pears in his house. Tariq bought 55 more pears at the store and 34 fewer corns than the number of pears. Find the combined total of the number of corns and pears Tariq has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Initial number of pears**: We know that Tariq initially has 48 pears.\n2. **Number of corns**: The problem states that there are five times as many corns as pears. Therefore, the initial number of corns can be calculated as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial number of corns} = 5 \\times \\text{Initial number of pears} = 5 \\times 48 = 240\n   \\]\n3. **Pears after buying more**: Tariq bought 55 more pears. So, the new total number of pears is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New total number of pears} = 48 + 55 = 103\n   \\]\n4. **Corn count after buying fewer**: Tariq bought 34 fewer corns than the number of pears he had after buying more. Therefore, the new number of corns is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New number of corns} = \\text{New total number of pears} - 34 = 103 - 34 = 69\n   \\]\n5. **Combined total of corns and pears**: Finally, to find the combined total of the number of corns and pears, we add the new number of corns and the new total number of pears:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Combined total} = \\text{New number of corns} + \\text{New total number of pears} = 69 + 103 = 172\n   \\]\n\nThus, the combined total of the number of corns and pears Tariq has in the house is 172.\n</think>\n<answer>172</answer>", "model_answer": 172.0, "ground_truth": 364.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any corn and pear, Tariq had 5 times as many corns as pears, which is 48 pears * 5 corn/pear = 240 corns\nThe total number of corns and pears that Tariq had before is 240 corns + 48 pears = 288 items\nWhen he bought 34 fewer corns than pears, he bought 55 corns - 34 corns = 21 corns\nIn total, he bought 21 corns + 55 pears = 76 items\nAfter the purchases, Tariq has 288 items + 76 items = 364 total corns and pears combined.\n#### 364"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 915, "question": "At Esteban's house, there are three times as many pears as figs. He has a total of 67 figs in his house. Esteban bought 48 more figs at the store and 38 fewer pears than the number of figs. Find the combined total of the number of pears and figs Esteban has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Esteban's house, there are three times as many pears as figs. He has a total of 67 figs in his house. Esteban bought 48 more figs at the store and 38 fewer pears than the number of figs. Find the combined total of the number of pears and figs Esteban has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Initial number of figs**: We know that Esteban initially has 67 figs.\n2. **Number of pears**: The problem states that there are three times as many pears as figs. Therefore, we can calculate the initial number of pears as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial number of pears} = 3 \\times \\text{Initial number of figs} = 3 \\times 67\n   \\]\n3. **Fig purchase**: Esteban bought 48 more figs at the store. So, the new total number of figs is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New total number of figs} = 67 + 48\n   \\]\n4. **Pears after purchase**: The problem states that Esteban bought 38 fewer pears than the number of figs he had after the purchase. So, the new number of pears is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New number of pears} = \\text{New total number of figs} - 38\n   \\]\n5. **Combined total**: Finally, we need to find the combined total of the number of pears and figs Esteban has in the house after the purchase.\n\nLet's calculate each step:\n\\[\n\\text{Initial number of pears} = 3 \\times 67 = 201\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{New total number of figs} = 67 + 48 = 115\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{New number of pears} = 115 - 38 = 77\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Combined total} = \\text{New total number of figs} + \\text{New number of pears} = 115 + 77\n\\]\n\nNow, let's perform the final addition:\n\\[\n115 + 77 = 192\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the combined total of the number of pears and figs Esteban has in the house is 192.\n</think>\n<answer>\n192\n</answer>", "model_answer": 192.0, "ground_truth": 326.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any pear and fig, Esteban had 3 times as many pears as figs, which is 67 figs * 3 pear/fig = 201 pears\nThe total number of pears and figs that Esteban had before is 201 pears + 67 figs = 268 items\nWhen he bought 38 fewer pears than figs, he bought 48 pears - 38 pears = 10 pears\nIn total, he bought 10 pears + 48 figs = 58 items\nAfter the purchases, Esteban has 268 items + 58 items = 326 total pears and figs combined.\n#### 326"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 916, "question": "At Santiago's house, there are three times as many corns as persimmons. He has a total of 50 persimmons in his house. Santiago bought 64 more persimmons at the store and 26 fewer corns than the number of persimmons. Find the combined total of the number of corns and persimmons Santiago has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Santiago's house, there are three times as many corns as persimmons. He has a total of 50 persimmons in his house. Santiago bought 64 more persimmons at the store and 26 fewer corns than the number of persimmons. Find the combined total of the number of corns and persimmons Santiago has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Initial Number of Persimmons**: Santiago initially has 50 persimmons.\n2. **Number of Corns**: According to the problem, there are three times as many corns as persimmons. Therefore, the initial number of corns is \\(3 \\times 50 = 150\\).\n3. **Persimmons After Purchase**: Santiago bought 64 more persimmons. So, the new total number of persimmons is \\(50 + 64 = 114\\).\n4. **Corns After Purchase**: Santiago bought 26 fewer corns than the number of persimmons he bought. Since he bought 64 persimmons, he bought \\(64 - 26 = 38\\) fewer corns. Therefore, the new number of corns is \\(150 - 38 = 112\\).\n5. **Combined Total**: To find the combined total of the number of corns and persimmons, we add the new number of corns and the new number of persimmons: \\(114 + 112 = 226\\).\n\nNow, let's verify the steps and the final answer.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe combined total of the number of corns and persimmons Santiago has in the house is 226.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 226.0, "ground_truth": 302.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any corn and persimmon, Santiago had 3 times as many corns as persimmons, which is 50 persimmons * 3 corn/persimmon = 150 corns\nThe total number of corns and persimmons that Santiago had before is 150 corns + 50 persimmons = 200 items\nWhen he bought 26 fewer corns than persimmons, he bought 64 corns - 26 corns = 38 corns\nIn total, he bought 38 corns + 64 persimmons = 102 items\nAfter the purchases, Santiago has 200 items + 102 items = 302 total corns and persimmons combined.\n#### 302"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 917, "question": "At Arash's house, there are six times as many pears as plums. He has a total of 47 plums in his house. Arash bought 70 more plums at the store and 21 fewer pears than the number of plums. Find the combined total of the number of pears and plums Arash has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Arash's house, there are six times as many pears as plums. He has a total of 47 plums in his house. Arash bought 70 more plums at the store and 21 fewer pears than the number of plums. Find the combined total of the number of pears and plums Arash has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Initial number of plums**: We are given that Arash initially has 47 plums.\n2. **Number of pears**: The problem states that there are six times as many pears as plums. Therefore, the initial number of pears can be calculated as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial number of pears} = 6 \\times \\text{Initial number of plums} = 6 \\times 47\n   \\]\n3. **Plums after buying more**: Arash bought 70 more plums at the store. So, the new number of plums is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New number of plums} = 47 + 70\n   \\]\n4. **Number of pears after buying fewer**: Arash bought 21 fewer pears than the number of plums he had initially. Therefore, the new number of pears is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New number of pears} = \\text{Initial number of pears} - 21\n   \\]\n5. **Calculate the initial number of pears**:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 47 = 282\n   \\]\n6. **Calculate the new number of plums**:\n   \\[\n   47 + 70 = 117\n   \\]\n7. **Calculate the new number of pears**:\n   \\[\n   282 - 21 = 261\n   \\]\n8. **Combined total of pears and plums**:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Combined total} = \\text{New number of pears} + \\text{New number of plums} = 261 + 117\n   \\]\n9. **Final calculation**:\n   \\[\n   261 + 117 = 378\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the combined total of the number of pears and plums Arash has in the house is 378.\n</think>\n<answer>378</answer>", "model_answer": 378.0, "ground_truth": 448.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any pear and plum, Arash had 6 times as many pears as plums, which is 47 plums * 6 pear/plum = 282 pears\nThe total number of pears and plums that Arash had before is 282 pears + 47 plums = 329 items\nWhen he bought 21 fewer pears than plums, he bought 70 pears - 21 pears = 49 pears\nIn total, he bought 49 pears + 70 plums = 119 items\nAfter the purchases, Arash has 329 items + 119 items = 448 total pears and plums combined.\n#### 448"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 918, "question": "At Sanjay's house, there are four times as many kiwis as figs. He has a total of 30 figs in his house. Sanjay bought 67 more figs at the store and 36 fewer kiwis than the number of figs. Find the combined total of the number of kiwis and figs Sanjay has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Sanjay's house, there are four times as many kiwis as figs. He has a total of 30 figs in his house. Sanjay bought 67 more figs at the store and 36 fewer kiwis than the number of figs. Find the combined total of the number of kiwis and figs Sanjay has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Initial number of figs**: We know that Sanjay initially has 30 figs.\n2. **Number of kiwis**: The problem states that there are four times as many kiwis as figs. Therefore, the initial number of kiwis can be calculated as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial number of kiwis} = 4 \\times \\text{Initial number of figs} = 4 \\times 30 = 120\n   \\]\n3. **Fig purchase**: Sanjay bought 67 more figs at the store. So, the new total number of figs is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New total number of figs} = \\text{Initial number of figs} + 67 = 30 + 67 = 97\n   \\]\n4. **Kiwis after purchase**: The problem states that Sanjay bought 36 fewer kiwis than the number of figs he bought. Since he bought 67 figs, the number of kiwis he bought is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of kiwis bought} = 67 - 36 = 31\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the new total number of kiwis is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New total number of kiwis} = \\text{Initial number of kiwis} + 31 = 120 + 31 = 151\n   \\]\n5. **Combined total**: Finally, to find the combined total of the number of kiwis and figs, we add the new total number of kiwis and the new total number of figs:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Combined total} = \\text{New total number of kiwis} + \\text{New total number of figs} = 151 + 97 = 248\n   \\]\n\nSo, the combined total of the number of kiwis and figs Sanjay has in the house is 248.\n</think>\n<answer>248</answer>", "model_answer": 248.0, "ground_truth": 248.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any kiwi and fig, Sanjay had 4 times as many kiwis as figs, which is 30 figs * 4 kiwi/fig = 120 kiwis\nThe total number of kiwis and figs that Sanjay had before is 120 kiwis + 30 figs = 150 items\nWhen he bought 36 fewer kiwis than figs, he bought 67 kiwis - 36 kiwis = 31 kiwis\nIn total, he bought 31 kiwis + 67 figs = 98 items\nAfter the purchases, Sanjay has 150 items + 98 items = 248 total kiwis and figs combined.\n#### 248"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 919, "question": "At Ravi's house, there are ten times as many persimmons as grapes. He has a total of 31 grapes in his house. Ravi bought 44 more grapes at the store and 24 fewer persimmons than the number of grapes. Find the combined total of the number of persimmons and grapes Ravi has in the house?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. At Ravi's house, there are ten times as many persimmons as grapes. He has a total of 31 grapes in his house. Ravi bought 44 more grapes at the store and 24 fewer persimmons than the number of grapes. Find the combined total of the number of persimmons and grapes Ravi has in the house?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach to determine the number of persimmons and grapes Ravi has in his house. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Initial Number of Grapes:**\n   - Ravi initially has 31 grapes.\n\n2. **Number of Persimmons:**\n   - According to the problem, there are ten times as many persimmons as grapes.\n   - Therefore, the initial number of persimmons is \\( 10 \\times 31 = 310 \\).\n\n3. **Additional Grapes Purchased:**\n   - Ravi bought 44 more grapes at the store.\n   - The new total number of grapes is \\( 31 + 44 = 75 \\).\n\n4. **Number of Persimmons After Purchase:**\n   - Ravi bought 24 fewer persimmons than the number of grapes he had after purchasing.\n   - The number of persimmons after the purchase is \\( 75 - 24 = 51 \\).\n\n5. **Combined Total of Persimmons and Grapes:**\n   - To find the combined total, we add the new number of grapes and the new number of persimmons.\n   - Combined total = \\( 75 + 51 = 126 \\).\n\nThus, the combined total of the number of persimmons and grapes Ravi has in his house is 126.\n</think>\n<answer>\n126\n</answer>", "model_answer": 126.0, "ground_truth": 405.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Before buying any persimmon and grape, Ravi had 10 times as many persimmons as grapes, which is 31 grapes * 10 persimmon/grape = 310 persimmons\nThe total number of persimmons and grapes that Ravi had before is 310 persimmons + 31 grapes = 341 items\nWhen he bought 24 fewer persimmons than grapes, he bought 44 persimmons - 24 persimmons = 20 persimmons\nIn total, he bought 20 persimmons + 44 grapes = 64 items\nAfter the purchases, Ravi has 341 items + 64 items = 405 total persimmons and grapes combined.\n#### 405"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 920, "question": "Luis slew 350 orcs with his mighty mace, while Jamal, using a lance, slew 4/5 as many orcs as Luis. Using a wooden club, DeShawn slew ten times as many orcs as Jamal. But Andre, having forgotten his axe at home, slew 2/4 as many orcs as DeShawn using a butter knife. How many orcs has Andre slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Luis slew 350 orcs with his mighty mace, while Jamal, using a lance, slew 4/5 as many orcs as Luis. Using a wooden club, DeShawn slew ten times as many orcs as Jamal. But Andre, having forgotten his axe at home, slew 2/4 as many orcs as DeShawn using a butter knife. How many orcs has Andre slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given relationships step by step and calculate the number of orcs slain by each person. Let's start with the information provided:\n\n1. Luis slew 350 orcs.\n2. Jamal slew \\( \\frac{4}{5} \\) as many orcs as Luis.\n3. DeShawn slew ten times as many orcs as Jamal.\n4. Andre slew \\( \\frac{2}{4} \\) as many orcs as DeShawn.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of orcs slain by Jamal:\n\\[ \\text{Jamal's orcs} = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 350 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of orcs slain by DeShawn:\n\\[ \\text{DeShawn's orcs} = 10 \\times \\text{Jamal's orcs} \\]\n\nThen, we calculate the number of orcs slain by Andre:\n\\[ \\text{Andre's orcs} = \\frac{2}{4} \\times \\text{DeShawn's orcs} \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate Jamal's orcs:\n\\[ \\text{Jamal's orcs} = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 350 = \\frac{4 \\times 350}{5} = \\frac{1400}{5} = 280 \\]\n\nNext, calculate DeShawn's orcs:\n\\[ \\text{DeShawn's orcs} = 10 \\times 280 = 2800 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate Andre's orcs:\n\\[ \\text{Andre's orcs} = \\frac{2}{4} \\times 2800 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2800 = 1400 \\]\n\nTherefore, Andre has slain 1400 orcs.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 1400.0, "ground_truth": 1400.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Jamal slew 4/5 as many orcs as Luis, or 350*4/5=280 orcs.\nDeShawn slew 10 as many orcs as Jamal, or 10*280=2800 orcs.\nAndre slew 1/2 as many orcs as DeShawn, or 2800*1/2=1400 orcs.\n#### 1400"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 921, "question": "Xavier slew 500 giants with his mighty mace, while Jamal, using a lance, slew 2/4 as many giants as Xavier. Using a wooden club, Sanjay slew five times as many giants as Jamal. But Benjamin, having forgotten his axe at home, slew 2/4 as many giants as Sanjay using a butter knife. How many giants has Benjamin slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Xavier slew 500 giants with his mighty mace, while Jamal, using a lance, slew 2/4 as many giants as Xavier. Using a wooden club, Sanjay slew five times as many giants as Jamal. But Benjamin, having forgotten his axe at home, slew 2/4 as many giants as Sanjay using a butter knife. How many giants has Benjamin slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given relationships step by step and calculate the number of giants slain by each person. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Xavier's Giants Slain**: We are given that Xavier slew 500 giants.\n   - \\( X = 500 \\)\n\n2. **Jamal's Giants Slain**: Jamal slew \\( \\frac{2}{4} \\) as many giants as Xavier.\n   - \\( J = \\frac{2}{4} \\times X \\)\n   - Simplify \\( \\frac{2}{4} \\) to \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n   - \\( J = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 500 \\)\n   - \\( J = 250 \\)\n\n3. **Sanjay's Giants Slain**: Sanjay slew five times as many giants as Jamal.\n   - \\( S = 5 \\times J \\)\n   - Substitute \\( J = 250 \\):\n   - \\( S = 5 \\times 250 \\)\n   - \\( S = 1250 \\)\n\n4. **Benjamin's Giants Slain**: Benjamin slew \\( \\frac{2}{4} \\) as many giants as Sanjay.\n   - \\( B = \\frac{2}{4} \\times S \\)\n   - Simplify \\( \\frac{2}{4} \\) to \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n   - \\( B = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1250 \\)\n   - \\( B = 625 \\)\n\nTherefore, Benjamin has slain 625 giants.\n</think>\n<answer>\nBenjamin has slain 625 giants.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 625.0, "ground_truth": 625.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Jamal slew 1/2 as many giants as Xavier, or 500*1/2=250 giants.\nSanjay slew 5 as many giants as Jamal, or 5*250=1250 giants.\nBenjamin slew 1/2 as many giants as Sanjay, or 1250*1/2=625 giants.\n#### 625"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 922, "question": "Ravi slew 300 ogres with his mighty sword, while Gabriel, using a spear, slew one-fourth as many ogres as Ravi. Using a rusty iron axe, Oliver slew eight times as many ogres as Gabriel. But Yuki, having forgotten his sword at home, slew 1/5 as many ogres as Oliver using a nail file. How many ogres has Yuki slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Ravi slew 300 ogres with his mighty sword, while Gabriel, using a spear, slew one-fourth as many ogres as Ravi. Using a rusty iron axe, Oliver slew eight times as many ogres as Gabriel. But Yuki, having forgotten his sword at home, slew 1/5 as many ogres as Oliver using a nail file. How many ogres has Yuki slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given information step by step and calculate the number of ogres slain by each person. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Ravi's Ogres Slain**: We are given that Ravi slew 300 ogres.\n   - \\( Ravi = 300 \\)\n\n2. **Gabriel's Ogres Slain**: Gabriel slew one-fourth as many ogres as Ravi.\n   - \\( Gabriel = \\frac{1}{4} \\times Ravi \\)\n   - \\( Gabriel = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 300 \\)\n   - \\( Gabriel = 75 \\)\n\n3. **Oliver's Ogres Slain**: Oliver slew eight times as many ogres as Gabriel.\n   - \\( Oliver = 8 \\times Gabriel \\)\n   - \\( Oliver = 8 \\times 75 \\)\n   - \\( Oliver = 600 \\)\n\n4. **Yuki's Ogres Slain**: Yuki slew 1/5 as many ogres as Oliver.\n   - \\( Yuki = \\frac{1}{5} \\times Oliver \\)\n   - \\( Yuki = \\frac{1}{5} \\times 600 \\)\n   - \\( Yuki = 120 \\)\n\nThus, the number of ogres Yuki has slain is 120.\n</think>\n<answer>\nYuki has slain 120 ogres.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 120.0, "ground_truth": 120.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Gabriel slew 1/4 as many ogres as Ravi, or 300*1/4=75 ogres.\nOliver slew 8 as many ogres as Gabriel, or 8*75=600 ogres.\nYuki slew 1/5 as many ogres as Oliver, or 600*1/5=120 ogres.\n#### 120"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 923, "question": "Gabriel slew 100 ogres with his mighty battle axe, while Kai, using a javelin, slew 1/10 as many ogres as Gabriel. Using a stone hammer, Liam slew five times as many ogres as Kai. But Jin, having forgotten his mace at home, slew 1/10 as many ogres as Liam using a wooden spoon. How many ogres has Jin slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Gabriel slew 100 ogres with his mighty battle axe, while Kai, using a javelin, slew 1/10 as many ogres as Gabriel. Using a stone hammer, Liam slew five times as many ogres as Kai. But Jin, having forgotten his mace at home, slew 1/10 as many ogres as Liam using a wooden spoon. How many ogres has Jin slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given information step by step and calculate the number of ogres slain by each person. Let's start with Gabriel, who slew 100 ogres. We'll use this information to find out how many ogres Kai, Liam, and Jin slew.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFrom the information given, we know that Gabriel slew 100 ogres. Now, let's calculate how many ogres Kai slew. According to the problem, Kai slew 1/10 as many ogres as Gabriel. Therefore, we can calculate the number of ogres Kai slew as follows:\nKai's ogres = Gabriel's ogres / 10\nKai's ogres = 100 / 10\nKai's ogres = 10\nNext, we need to find out how many ogres Liam slew. The problem states that Liam slew five times as many ogres as Kai. So, we can calculate the number of ogres Liam slew as follows:\nLiam's ogres = Kai's ogres * 5\nLiam's ogres = 10 * 5\nLiam's ogres = 50\nFinally, we need to determine how many ogres Jin slew. The problem mentions that Jin slew 1/10 as many ogres as Liam. Therefore, we can calculate the number of ogres Jin slew as follows:\nJin's ogres = Liam's ogres / 10\nJin's ogres = 50 / 10\nJin's ogres = 5\nSo, Jin slew 5 ogres.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 5.0, "ground_truth": 5.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Kai slew 1/10 as many ogres as Gabriel, or 100*1/10=10 ogres.\nLiam slew 5 as many ogres as Kai, or 5*10=50 ogres.\nJin slew 1/10 as many ogres as Liam, or 50*1/10=5 ogres.\n#### 5"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 924, "question": "Jin slew 50 trolls with his mighty sword, while Umar, using a spear, slew four-fifths as many trolls as Jin. Using a rusty iron axe, Qasim slew two times as many trolls as Umar. But DeShawn, having forgotten his sword at home, slew 1/5 as many trolls as Qasim using a nail file. How many trolls has DeShawn slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Jin slew 50 trolls with his mighty sword, while Umar, using a spear, slew four-fifths as many trolls as Jin. Using a rusty iron axe, Qasim slew two times as many trolls as Umar. But DeShawn, having forgotten his sword at home, slew 1/5 as many trolls as Qasim using a nail file. How many trolls has DeShawn slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given information step by step and calculate the number of trolls slain by each person. Let's start with Jin and work our way down to DeShawn.\n\n1. **Jin's Trolls:**\n   - Jin slew 50 trolls.\n   - \\( \\text{Trolls slain by Jin} = 50 \\)\n\n2. **Umar's Trolls:**\n   - Umar slew four-fifths as many trolls as Jin.\n   - \\( \\text{Trolls slain by Umar} = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 50 \\)\n   - Calculate the fraction: \\( \\frac{4}{5} \\times 50 = 4 \\times 10 = 40 \\)\n   - \\( \\text{Trolls slain by Umar} = 40 \\)\n\n3. **Qasim's Trolls:**\n   - Qasim slew two times as many trolls as Umar.\n   - \\( \\text{Trolls slain by Qasim} = 2 \\times 40 \\)\n   - \\( \\text{Trolls slain by Qasim} = 80 \\)\n\n4. **DeShawn's Trolls:**\n   - DeShawn slew 1/5 as many trolls as Qasim.\n   - \\( \\text{Trolls slain by DeShawn} = \\frac{1}{5} \\times 80 \\)\n   - Calculate the fraction: \\( \\frac{1}{5} \\times 80 = 16 \\)\n   - \\( \\text{Trolls slain by DeShawn} = 16 \\)\n\nTherefore, DeShawn has slain 16 trolls.\n</think>\n<answer>\nDeShawn has slain 16 trolls.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 16.0, "ground_truth": 16.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Umar slew 4/5 as many trolls as Jin, or 50*4/5=40 trolls.\nQasim slew 2 as many trolls as Umar, or 2*40=80 trolls.\nDeShawn slew 1/5 as many trolls as Qasim, or 80*1/5=16 trolls.\n#### 16"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 925, "question": "Oscar slew 450 giants with his mighty war hammer, while Diego, using a halberd, slew four-fifths as many giants as Oscar. Using a bone dagger, DeShawn slew four times as many giants as Diego. But Arash, having forgotten his hammer at home, slew one-half as many giants as DeShawn using a feather. How many giants has Arash slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Oscar slew 450 giants with his mighty war hammer, while Diego, using a halberd, slew four-fifths as many giants as Oscar. Using a bone dagger, DeShawn slew four times as many giants as Diego. But Arash, having forgotten his hammer at home, slew one-half as many giants as DeShawn using a feather. How many giants has Arash slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given relationships step by step and calculate the number of giants slain by each person. Let's start with the information provided:\n\n1. Oscar slew 450 giants.\n2. Diego slew four-fifths as many giants as Oscar.\n3. DeShawn slew four times as many giants as Diego.\n4. Arash slew one-half as many giants as DeShawn.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of giants Diego slew:\n\\[ \\text{Diego's giants} = \\frac{4}{5} \\times \\text{Oscar's giants} = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 450 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of giants DeShawn slew:\n\\[ \\text{DeShawn's giants} = 4 \\times \\text{Diego's giants} = 4 \\times \\left( \\frac{4}{5} \\times 450 \\right) \\]\n\nThen, we calculate the number of giants Arash slew:\n\\[ \\text{Arash's giants} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{DeShawn's giants} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\left( 4 \\times \\left( \\frac{4}{5} \\times 450 \\right) \\right) \\]\n\nLet's perform the calculations step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate Diego's giants:\n\\[ \\text{Diego's giants} = \\frac{4}{5} \\times 450 = \\frac{4 \\times 450}{5} = \\frac{1800}{5} = 360 \\]\n\nNext, calculate DeShawn's giants:\n\\[ \\text{DeShawn's giants} = 4 \\times 360 = 1440 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate Arash's giants:\n\\[ \\text{Arash's giants} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1440 = \\frac{1440}{2} = 720 \\]\n\nTherefore, Arash slew 720 giants.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 720.0, "ground_truth": 720.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Diego slew 4/5 as many giants as Oscar, or 450*4/5=360 giants.\nDeShawn slew 4 as many giants as Diego, or 4*360=1440 giants.\nArash slew 1/2 as many giants as DeShawn, or 1440*1/2=720 giants.\n#### 720"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 926, "question": "Luis slew 400 orcs with his mighty war hammer, while DeShawn, using a halberd, slew 3/4 as many orcs as Luis. Using a bone dagger, Alejandro slew six times as many orcs as DeShawn. But Wei, having forgotten his hammer at home, slew two-sixths as many orcs as Alejandro using a feather. How many orcs has Wei slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Luis slew 400 orcs with his mighty war hammer, while DeShawn, using a halberd, slew 3/4 as many orcs as Luis. Using a bone dagger, Alejandro slew six times as many orcs as DeShawn. But Wei, having forgotten his hammer at home, slew two-sixths as many orcs as Alejandro using a feather. How many orcs has Wei slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given relationships step by step and calculate the number of orcs slain by each person. Let's start with the information provided:\n\n1. Luis slew 400 orcs.\n2. DeShawn slew \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\) as many orcs as Luis.\n3. Alejandro slew six times as many orcs as DeShawn.\n4. Wei slew two-sixths as many orcs as Alejandro.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of orcs DeShawn slew:\n\\[ \\text{DeShawn's orcs} = \\frac{3}{4} \\times 400 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of orcs Alejandro slew:\n\\[ \\text{Alejandro's orcs} = 6 \\times \\text{DeShawn's orcs} \\]\n\nThen, we calculate the number of orcs Wei slew:\n\\[ \\text{Wei's orcs} = \\frac{2}{6} \\times \\text{Alejandro's orcs} \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate DeShawn's orcs:\n\\[ \\text{DeShawn's orcs} = \\frac{3}{4} \\times 400 = 300 \\]\n\nNext, calculate Alejandro's orcs:\n\\[ \\text{Alejandro's orcs} = 6 \\times 300 = 1800 \\]\n\nThen, calculate Wei's orcs:\n\\[ \\text{Wei's orcs} = \\frac{2}{6} \\times 1800 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 1800 = 600 \\]\n\nTherefore, Wei slew 600 orcs.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 600.0, "ground_truth": 600.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "DeShawn slew 3/4 as many orcs as Luis, or 400*3/4=300 orcs.\nAlejandro slew 6 as many orcs as DeShawn, or 6*300=1800 orcs.\nWei slew 1/3 as many orcs as Alejandro, or 1800*1/3=600 orcs.\n#### 600"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 927, "question": "James slew 450 giants with his mighty sword, while Yuki, using a spear, slew 1/10 as many giants as James. Using a rusty iron axe, Sanjay slew five times as many giants as Yuki. But Esteban, having forgotten his sword at home, slew one-fifth as many giants as Sanjay using a nail file. How many giants has Esteban slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. James slew 450 giants with his mighty sword, while Yuki, using a spear, slew 1/10 as many giants as James. Using a rusty iron axe, Sanjay slew five times as many giants as Yuki. But Esteban, having forgotten his sword at home, slew one-fifth as many giants as Sanjay using a nail file. How many giants has Esteban slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given information step by step and calculate the number of giants slain by each person. Let's start with the information provided:\n\n1. James slew 450 giants.\n2. Yuki slew 1/10 as many giants as James.\n3. Sanjay slew five times as many giants as Yuki.\n4. Esteban slew one-fifth as many giants as Sanjay.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of giants Yuki slew:\n\\[ \\text{Yuki's giants} = \\frac{1}{10} \\times 450 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of giants Sanjay slew:\n\\[ \\text{Sanjay's giants} = 5 \\times \\text{Yuki's giants} \\]\n\nThen, we calculate the number of giants Esteban slew:\n\\[ \\text{Esteban's giants} = \\frac{1}{5} \\times \\text{Sanjay's giants} \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate Yuki's giants:\n\\[ \\text{Yuki's giants} = \\frac{1}{10} \\times 450 = 45 \\]\n\nNext, calculate Sanjay's giants:\n\\[ \\text{Sanjay's giants} = 5 \\times 45 = 225 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate Esteban's giants:\n\\[ \\text{Esteban's giants} = \\frac{1}{5} \\times 225 = 45 \\]\n\nTherefore, Esteban has slain 45 giants.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 45.0, "ground_truth": 45.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Yuki slew 1/10 as many giants as James, or 450*1/10=45 giants.\nSanjay slew 5 as many giants as Yuki, or 5*45=225 giants.\nEsteban slew 1/5 as many giants as Sanjay, or 225*1/5=45 giants.\n#### 45"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 928, "question": "Gabriel slew 400 trolls with his mighty sword, while Carlos, using a spear, slew three-sixths as many trolls as Gabriel. Using a rusty iron axe, Ali slew four times as many trolls as Carlos. But Wei, having forgotten his sword at home, slew 1/8 as many trolls as Ali using a nail file. How many trolls has Wei slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Gabriel slew 400 trolls with his mighty sword, while Carlos, using a spear, slew three-sixths as many trolls as Gabriel. Using a rusty iron axe, Ali slew four times as many trolls as Carlos. But Wei, having forgotten his sword at home, slew 1/8 as many trolls as Ali using a nail file. How many trolls has Wei slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given relationships step by step and calculate the number of trolls slain by each person. Let's start with the information provided:\n\n1. Gabriel slew 400 trolls.\n2. Carlos slew three-sixths as many trolls as Gabriel.\n3. Ali slew four times as many trolls as Carlos.\n4. Wei slew one-eighth as many trolls as Ali.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of trolls slain by Carlos:\n\\[ \\text{Carlos' trolls} = \\frac{3}{6} \\times 400 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of trolls slain by Ali:\n\\[ \\text{Ali's trolls} = 4 \\times \\text{Carlos' trolls} \\]\n\nThen, we calculate the number of trolls slain by Wei:\n\\[ \\text{Wei's trolls} = \\frac{1}{8} \\times \\text{Ali's trolls} \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate the number of trolls slain by Carlos:\n\\[ \\text{Carlos' trolls} = \\frac{3}{6} \\times 400 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 400 = 200 \\]\n\nNext, calculate the number of trolls slain by Ali:\n\\[ \\text{Ali's trolls} = 4 \\times 200 = 800 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate the number of trolls slain by Wei:\n\\[ \\text{Wei's trolls} = \\frac{1}{8} \\times 800 = 100 \\]\n\nTherefore, Wei has slain 100 trolls.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 100.0, "ground_truth": 100.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Carlos slew 1/2 as many trolls as Gabriel, or 400*1/2=200 trolls.\nAli slew 4 as many trolls as Carlos, or 4*200=800 trolls.\nWei slew 1/8 as many trolls as Ali, or 800*1/8=100 trolls.\n#### 100"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 929, "question": "Santiago slew 100 goblins with his mighty sword, while Gabriel, using a spear, slew three-fifths as many goblins as Santiago. Using a rusty iron axe, Ali slew three times as many goblins as Gabriel. But Ravi, having forgotten his sword at home, slew two-fourths as many goblins as Ali using a nail file. How many goblins has Ravi slain?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Santiago slew 100 goblins with his mighty sword, while Gabriel, using a spear, slew three-fifths as many goblins as Santiago. Using a rusty iron axe, Ali slew three times as many goblins as Gabriel. But Ravi, having forgotten his sword at home, slew two-fourths as many goblins as Ali using a nail file. How many goblins has Ravi slain?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the steps given and calculate the number of goblins slain by each person sequentially. Let's break it down:\n\n1. Santiago slew 100 goblins.\n2. Gabriel slew three-fifths as many goblins as Santiago.\n3. Ali slew three times as many goblins as Gabriel.\n4. Ravi slew two-fourths as many goblins as Ali.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of goblins Gabriel slew:\n\\[ \\text{Gabriel's goblins} = \\frac{3}{5} \\times 100 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of goblins Ali slew:\n\\[ \\text{Ali's goblins} = 3 \\times \\text{Gabriel's goblins} \\]\n\nThen, we calculate the number of goblins Ravi slew:\n\\[ \\text{Ravi's goblins} = \\frac{2}{4} \\times \\text{Ali's goblins} \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate Gabriel's goblins:\n\\[ \\text{Gabriel's goblins} = \\frac{3}{5} \\times 100 = 60 \\]\n\nNext, calculate Ali's goblins:\n\\[ \\text{Ali's goblins} = 3 \\times 60 = 180 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate Ravi's goblins:\n\\[ \\text{Ravi's goblins} = \\frac{2}{4} \\times 180 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 180 = 90 \\]\n\nTherefore, Ravi has slain 90 goblins.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 90.0, "ground_truth": 90.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "Gabriel slew 3/5 as many goblins as Santiago, or 100*3/5=60 goblins.\nAli slew 3 as many goblins as Gabriel, or 3*60=180 goblins.\nRavi slew 1/2 as many goblins as Ali, or 180*1/2=90 goblins.\n#### 90"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 930, "question": "When the water is cold Leilani swims a kilometer in 15 minutes. When the water is warm Leilani swims a kilometer in 3 minutes more than thrice as long. How much longer does Leilani take to swim 6 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Leilani swims a kilometer in 15 minutes. When the water is warm Leilani swims a kilometer in 3 minutes more than thrice as long. How much longer does Leilani take to swim 6 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to first determine the time it takes Leilani to swim a kilometer in both cold and warm water conditions. Then, we can calculate the total time for 6 kilometers in both conditions and find the difference. Let's break it down step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, let's denote the time it takes Leilani to swim a kilometer in cold water as \\( T_{\\text{cold}} \\). According to the problem, \\( T_{\\text{cold}} = 15 \\) minutes.\n\nFor warm water, the time it takes Leilani to swim a kilometer is 3 minutes more than thrice the time it takes in cold water. We can express this as:\n\\[ T_{\\text{warm}} = 3 \\times T_{\\text{cold}} + 3 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( T_{\\text{cold}} = 15 \\) minutes into the equation:\n\\[ T_{\\text{warm}} = 3 \\times 15 + 3 = 45 + 3 = 48 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nNow, we need to calculate the total time it takes Leilani to swim 6 kilometers in both conditions.\n\nFor cold water:\n\\[ \\text{Total time for 6 km in cold water} = 6 \\times T_{\\text{cold}} = 6 \\times 15 = 90 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nFor warm water:\n\\[ \\text{Total time for 6 km in warm water} = 6 \\times T_{\\text{warm}} = 6 \\times 48 = 288 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nFinally, we find the difference in time between swimming 6 kilometers in warm water and cold water:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} = \\text{Total time in warm water} - \\text{Total time in cold water} = 288 - 90 = 198 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, Leilani takes 198 minutes longer to swim 6 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 6.0, "ground_truth": 198.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water kilometer = 15 minutes\nWarm water kilometer = 3+3(15)=48 minutes\n6 kilometers in cold water: 6(15)=90 minutes\n6 kilometers in warm water: 6(48)=288 minutes\nLeilani takes 288-90=198 minutes longer\n#### 198"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 931, "question": "When the water is cold Andre swims a kilometer in 27 minutes. When the water is warm Andre swims a kilometer in 3 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Andre take to swim 7 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Andre swims a kilometer in 27 minutes. When the water is warm Andre swims a kilometer in 3 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Andre take to swim 7 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to first determine the time it takes Andre to swim a kilometer in both cold and warm water conditions. Then, we can calculate the total time for 7 kilometers in both conditions and find the difference. Let's break it down step by step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, let's denote the time it takes Andre to swim a kilometer in cold water as \\( T_c \\) and in warm water as \\( T_w \\). According to the problem, \\( T_c = 27 \\) minutes. The problem also states that in warm water, Andre swims a kilometer in 3 minutes more than twice as long as in cold water. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ T_w = 2 \\cdot T_c + 3 \\]\nSubstituting \\( T_c = 27 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ T_w = 2 \\cdot 27 + 3 = 54 + 3 = 57 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to calculate the total time for 7 kilometers in both conditions. The total time for 7 kilometers in cold water is:\n\\[ 7 \\cdot T_c = 7 \\cdot 27 = 189 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nThe total time for 7 kilometers in warm water is:\n\\[ 7 \\cdot T_w = 7 \\cdot 57 = 399 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nFinally, to find out how much longer it takes Andre to swim 7 kilometers on a hot day compared to a cold day, we subtract the total time for cold water from the total time for warm water:\n\\[ 399 - 189 = 210 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Andre takes 210 minutes longer to swim 7 kilometers on a hot day than on a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 7.0, "ground_truth": 210.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water kilometer = 27 minutes\nWarm water kilometer = 3+2(27)=57 minutes\n7 kilometers in cold water: 7(27)=189 minutes\n7 kilometers in warm water: 7(57)=399 minutes\nAndre takes 399-189=210 minutes longer\n#### 210"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 932, "question": "When the water is cold Ava swims a kilometer in 18 minutes. When the water is warm Ava swims a kilometer in 7 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Ava take to swim 2 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Ava swims a kilometer in 18 minutes. When the water is warm Ava swims a kilometer in 7 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Ava take to swim 2 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to first determine the time it takes Ava to swim a kilometer in both cold and warm water conditions. Then, we can calculate the time it takes her to swim 2 kilometers in each condition and find the difference. Let's start by defining the variables and setting up the equations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time it takes Ava to swim a kilometer in cold water as \\( t_c \\) and in warm water as \\( t_w \\). According to the problem, \\( t_c = 18 \\) minutes. The problem also states that in warm water, Ava swims a kilometer in 7 minutes more than twice as long as in cold water. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ t_w = 2t_c + 7 \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( t_c \\) into the equation:\n\\[ t_w = 2(18) + 7 = 36 + 7 = 43 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to find the time it takes Ava to swim 2 kilometers in both conditions. Since the time to swim a kilometer is the same as the time to swim 2 kilometers, the time to swim 2 kilometers in cold water is:\n\\[ 2t_c = 2 \\times 18 = 36 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nAnd the time to swim 2 kilometers in warm water is:\n\\[ 2t_w = 2 \\times 43 = 86 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nThe difference in time between swimming 2 kilometers on a hot day and a cold day is:\n\\[ 86 - 36 = 50 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Ava takes 50 minutes longer to swim 2 kilometers on a hot day than on a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 2.0, "ground_truth": 50.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water kilometer = 18 minutes\nWarm water kilometer = 7+2(18)=43 minutes\n2 kilometers in cold water: 2(18)=36 minutes\n2 kilometers in warm water: 2(43)=86 minutes\nAva takes 86-36=50 minutes longer\n#### 50"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 933, "question": "When the water is cold Liam swims a mile in 26 minutes. When the water is warm Liam swims a mile in 1 minutes more than two times as long. How much longer does Liam take to swim 3 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Liam swims a mile in 26 minutes. When the water is warm Liam swims a mile in 1 minutes more than two times as long. How much longer does Liam take to swim 3 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to first determine the time it takes for Liam to swim a mile in both cold and warm water conditions. Then, we can calculate the total time for 3 miles in each condition and find the difference. Let's start by defining the variables and setting up the equations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time it takes for Liam to swim a mile in cold water as \\( T_c \\) and in warm water as \\( T_w \\). According to the problem, \\( T_c = 26 \\) minutes. The problem also states that in warm water, it takes 1 minute more than two times as long as in cold water. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ T_w = 2 \\cdot T_c + 1 \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( T_c \\):\n\\[ T_w = 2 \\cdot 26 + 1 = 52 + 1 = 53 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to find the total time it takes for Liam to swim 3 miles in both conditions:\n- For cold water: \\( 3 \\cdot T_c = 3 \\cdot 26 = 78 \\text{ minutes} \\)\n- For warm water: \\( 3 \\cdot T_w = 3 \\cdot 53 = 159 \\text{ minutes} \\)\nThe difference in time between swimming 3 miles in warm water and cold water is:\n\\[ 159 - 78 = 81 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Liam takes 81 minutes longer to swim 3 miles on a hot day than a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 81.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water mile = 26 minutes\nWarm water mile = 1+2(26)=53 minutes\n3 miles in cold water: 3(26)=78 minutes\n3 miles in warm water: 3(53)=159 minutes\nLiam takes 159-78=81 minutes longer\n#### 81"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 934, "question": "When the water is cold Sophia swims a mile in 24 minutes. When the water is warm Sophia swims a mile in 7 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Sophia take to swim 3 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Sophia swims a mile in 24 minutes. When the water is warm Sophia swims a mile in 7 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Sophia take to swim 3 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine how long it takes Sophia to swim a mile in both cold and warm water. Then, we'll calculate the time it takes for her to swim 3 miles in both conditions. Finally, we'll find the difference in time between swimming 3 miles in warm water and cold water. Let's start by defining the variables and setting up the equations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time it takes Sophia to swim a mile in cold water as \\( T_c \\) and in warm water as \\( T_w \\). According to the problem, \\( T_c = 24 \\) minutes. The problem also states that in warm water, it takes her 7 minutes more than twice as long as in cold water. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ T_w = 2 \\cdot T_c + 7 \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( T_c \\):\n\\[ T_w = 2 \\cdot 24 + 7 = 48 + 7 = 55 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to find out how long it takes Sophia to swim 3 miles in both conditions. For cold water:\n\\[ \\text{Time for 3 miles in cold water} = 3 \\cdot T_c = 3 \\cdot 24 = 72 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nFor warm water:\n\\[ \\text{Time for 3 miles in warm water} = 3 \\cdot T_w = 3 \\cdot 55 = 165 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nFinally, we need to find the difference in time between swimming 3 miles in warm water and cold water:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} = 165 - 72 = 93 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Sophia takes 93 minutes longer to swim 3 miles on a hot day than on a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 93.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water mile = 24 minutes\nWarm water mile = 7+2(24)=55 minutes\n3 miles in cold water: 3(24)=72 minutes\n3 miles in warm water: 3(55)=165 minutes\nSophia takes 165-72=93 minutes longer\n#### 93"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 935, "question": "When the water is cold Valeria swims a mile in 14 minutes. When the water is warm Valeria swims a mile in 3 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Valeria take to swim 2 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Valeria swims a mile in 14 minutes. When the water is warm Valeria swims a mile in 3 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Valeria take to swim 2 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine how long it takes Valeria to swim a mile in both cold and warm water. Then, we can calculate the time it takes for her to swim 2 miles in each condition. Finally, we can find the difference in time between swimming 2 miles in warm water and cold water. Let's start by defining the variables and setting up the equations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time it takes Valeria to swim a mile in cold water as \\( T_{\\text{cold}} \\) and in warm water as \\( T_{\\text{warm}} \\). According to the problem, \\( T_{\\text{cold}} = 14 \\) minutes. We are also given that \\( T_{\\text{warm}} = 2 \\times T_{\\text{cold}} + 3 \\). Substituting the value of \\( T_{\\text{cold}} \\) into this equation, we get:\n\\[ T_{\\text{warm}} = 2 \\times 14 + 3 = 28 + 3 = 31 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to find out how long it takes Valeria to swim 2 miles in both conditions. For cold water, the time to swim 2 miles is:\n\\[ 2 \\times T_{\\text{cold}} = 2 \\times 14 = 28 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nFor warm water, the time to swim 2 miles is:\n\\[ 2 \\times T_{\\text{warm}} = 2 \\times 31 = 62 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nThe difference in time between swimming 2 miles in warm water and cold water is:\n\\[ 62 - 28 = 34 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Valeria takes 34 minutes longer to swim 2 miles on a hot day than on a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 2.0, "ground_truth": 34.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water mile = 14 minutes\nWarm water mile = 3+2(14)=31 minutes\n2 miles in cold water: 2(14)=28 minutes\n2 miles in warm water: 2(31)=62 minutes\nValeria takes 62-28=34 minutes longer\n#### 34"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 936, "question": "When the water is cold Tariq swims a mile in 16 minutes. When the water is warm Tariq swims a mile in 6 minutes more than thrice as long. How much longer does Tariq take to swim 8 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Tariq swims a mile in 16 minutes. When the water is warm Tariq swims a mile in 6 minutes more than thrice as long. How much longer does Tariq take to swim 8 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to first determine the time Tariq takes to swim a mile in both cold and warm water conditions. Then, we can calculate the total time for 8 miles in both conditions and find the difference. Let's start by defining the variables and setting up the equations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time Tariq takes to swim a mile in cold water as \\( t_c \\) and in warm water as \\( t_w \\). According to the problem, \\( t_c = 16 \\) minutes. The problem also states that in warm water, Tariq swims a mile in 6 minutes more than thrice as long as in cold water. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ t_w = 3t_c + 6 \\]\nSubstituting \\( t_c = 16 \\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ t_w = 3(16) + 6 = 48 + 6 = 54 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to find the total time Tariq takes to swim 8 miles in both conditions. The total time for 8 miles in cold water is:\n\\[ 8t_c = 8 \\times 16 = 128 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nThe total time for 8 miles in warm water is:\n\\[ 8t_w = 8 \\times 54 = 432 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nThe difference in time between swimming 8 miles in warm water and cold water is:\n\\[ 432 - 128 = 304 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Tariq takes 304 minutes longer to swim 8 miles on a hot day than a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 8.0, "ground_truth": 304.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water mile = 16 minutes\nWarm water mile = 6+3(16)=54 minutes\n8 miles in cold water: 8(16)=128 minutes\n8 miles in warm water: 8(54)=432 minutes\nTariq takes 432-128=304 minutes longer\n#### 304"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 937, "question": "When the water is cold Xena swims a kilometer in 10 minutes. When the water is warm Xena swims a kilometer in 5 minutes more than four times as long. How much longer does Xena take to swim 5 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Xena swims a kilometer in 10 minutes. When the water is warm Xena swims a kilometer in 5 minutes more than four times as long. How much longer does Xena take to swim 5 kilometers on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to first determine the time it takes Xena to swim a kilometer in both cold and warm water conditions. Then, we can calculate the total time for 5 kilometers in each condition and find the difference. Let's start by defining the variables and setting up the equations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time it takes Xena to swim a kilometer in cold water as \\( t_c \\) and in warm water as \\( t_w \\). According to the problem, \\( t_c = 10 \\) minutes. The problem also states that in warm water, Xena swims a kilometer in 5 minutes more than four times as long as in cold water. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ t_w = 4t_c + 5 \\]\nSubstituting \\( t_c = 10 \\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ t_w = 4(10) + 5 = 40 + 5 = 45 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to find the total time it takes Xena to swim 5 kilometers in both conditions. The time to swim 5 kilometers in cold water is:\n\\[ 5t_c = 5 \\times 10 = 50 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nThe time to swim 5 kilometers in warm water is:\n\\[ 5t_w = 5 \\times 45 = 225 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nThe difference in time between swimming 5 kilometers in warm water and cold water is:\n\\[ 225 - 50 = 175 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Xena takes 175 minutes longer to swim 5 kilometers on a hot day than on a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 5.0, "ground_truth": 175.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water kilometer = 10 minutes\nWarm water kilometer = 5+4(10)=45 minutes\n5 kilometers in cold water: 5(10)=50 minutes\n5 kilometers in warm water: 5(45)=225 minutes\nXena takes 225-50=175 minutes longer\n#### 175"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 938, "question": "When the water is cold Aisha swims a mile in 10 minutes. When the water is warm Aisha swims a mile in 6 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Aisha take to swim 3 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Aisha swims a mile in 10 minutes. When the water is warm Aisha swims a mile in 6 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Aisha take to swim 3 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into several steps. First, we need to determine how long it takes Aisha to swim a mile in both cold and warm water. Then, we'll calculate the time it takes for her to swim 3 miles in each condition. Finally, we'll find the difference in time between swimming 3 miles in warm water and cold water. Let's start with the first step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time it takes Aisha to swim a mile in cold water as \\( T_{\\text{cold}} \\). According to the problem, \\( T_{\\text{cold}} = 10 \\) minutes.\n\nFor warm water, the time it takes Aisha to swim a mile is \\( T_{\\text{warm}} = 2 \\times T_{\\text{cold}} + 6 \\). Substituting \\( T_{\\text{cold}} = 10 \\) minutes, we get:\n\\[ T_{\\text{warm}} = 2 \\times 10 + 6 = 26 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nNow, we need to calculate the time it takes Aisha to swim 3 miles in both conditions.\n\nFor cold water, the time to swim 3 miles is:\n\\[ \\text{Time}_{\\text{cold}} = 3 \\times T_{\\text{cold}} = 3 \\times 10 = 30 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nFor warm water, the time to swim 3 miles is:\n\\[ \\text{Time}_{\\text{warm}} = 3 \\times T_{\\text{warm}} = 3 \\times 26 = 78 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nFinally, we find the difference in time between swimming 3 miles in warm water and cold water:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} = \\text{Time}_{\\text{warm}} - \\text{Time}_{\\text{cold}} = 78 - 30 = 48 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, Aisha takes 48 minutes longer to swim 3 miles on a hot day than on a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 48.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water mile = 10 minutes\nWarm water mile = 6+2(10)=26 minutes\n3 miles in cold water: 3(10)=30 minutes\n3 miles in warm water: 3(26)=78 minutes\nAisha takes 78-30=48 minutes longer\n#### 48"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 939, "question": "When the water is cold Tariq swims a mile in 25 minutes. When the water is warm Tariq swims a mile in 1 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Tariq take to swim 9 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. When the water is cold Tariq swims a mile in 25 minutes. When the water is warm Tariq swims a mile in 1 minutes more than twice as long. How much longer does Tariq take to swim 9 miles on a hot day than a cold day?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to first determine the time Tariq takes to swim a mile in both cold and warm water conditions. Then, we can calculate the total time for 9 miles in both conditions and find the difference. Let's start by defining the variables and setting up the equations.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's denote the time Tariq takes to swim a mile in cold water as \\( t_c \\) and in warm water as \\( t_w \\). According to the problem, \\( t_c = 25 \\) minutes. The problem also states that in warm water, Tariq swims a mile in 1 minute more than twice as long as in cold water. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ t_w = 2t_c + 1 \\]\nSubstituting the value of \\( t_c \\):\n\\[ t_w = 2 \\times 25 + 1 = 50 + 1 = 51 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nNow, we need to find the total time Tariq takes to swim 9 miles in both conditions:\n- In cold water: \\( 9 \\times t_c = 9 \\times 25 = 225 \\text{ minutes} \\)\n- In warm water: \\( 9 \\times t_w = 9 \\times 51 = 459 \\text{ minutes} \\)\nThe difference in time between swimming 9 miles in warm water and cold water is:\n\\[ 459 - 225 = 234 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nTherefore, Tariq takes 234 minutes longer to swim 9 miles on a hot day than a cold day.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 9.0, "ground_truth": 234.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "Cold water mile = 25 minutes\nWarm water mile = 1+2(25)=51 minutes\n9 miles in cold water: 9(25)=225 minutes\n9 miles in warm water: 9(51)=459 minutes\nTariq takes 459-225=234 minutes longer\n#### 234"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 940, "question": "375 people apply for a job at Facebook. Of the people that apply, only 40% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 10% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, four-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 375 people apply for a job at Facebook. Of the people that apply, only 40% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 10% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, four-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the steps sequentially and calculate the number of people at each stage. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Total applicants**: 375 people\n2. **Interviews**: 40% of the applicants receive interviews.\n3. **Job offers**: 10% of those who receive interviews get a job offer.\n4. **Acceptance**: Four-sixths of those who receive a job offer accept the position.\n\nLet's calculate each step:\n\n1. **Interviews**:\n   - Number of applicants = 375\n   - Percentage receiving interviews = 40%\n   - Number of interviews = 375 * 0.40\n\n2. **Job offers**:\n   - Number of interviews = 375 * 0.40\n   - Percentage receiving job offers = 10%\n   - Number of job offers = (375 * 0.40) * 0.10\n\n3. **Acceptance**:\n   - Number of job offers = (375 * 0.40) * 0.10\n   - Percentage accepting the offer = 4/6\n   - Number of acceptances = (375 * 0.40 * 0.10) * (4/6)\n\nNow, let's perform the calculations:\n\n1. **Interviews**:\n   - Number of interviews = 375 * 0.40 = 150\n\n2. **Job offers**:\n   - Number of job offers = 150 * 0.10 = 15\n\n3. **Acceptance**:\n   - Number of acceptances = (150 * 0.10) * (4/6) = 15 * (4/6) = 15 * 2/3 = 10\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 10.\n</think>\n<answer>10 people accept the position.</answer>", "model_answer": 10.0, "ground_truth": 10.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 375 * 0.4 = 150 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 150 * 0.1 = 15 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 15 * 2/3 = 10 people\n#### 10"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 941, "question": "475 people apply for a job at Apple. Of the people that apply, only 48% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 25% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, one-third of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 475 people apply for a job at Apple. Of the people that apply, only 48% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 25% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, one-third of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach, breaking down the percentages and applying them sequentially to the initial number of applicants. Let's start with the given information:\n1. Total applicants: 475\n2. Percentage receiving interviews: 48%\n3. Percentage receiving job offers from those interviewed: 25%\n4. Percentage of job offers accepted: 1/3\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of applicants who receive interviews:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants receiving interviews} = 475 \\times 0.48 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of applicants who receive job offers from those interviewed:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants receiving job offers} = (\\text{Applicants receiving interviews}) \\times 0.25 \\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the number of job offers accepted:\n\\[ \\text{Job offers accepted} = (\\text{Applicants receiving job offers}) \\times \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step-by-step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate the number of applicants who receive interviews:\n\\[ 475 \\times 0.48 = 226 \\]\n\nNext, calculate the number of applicants who receive job offers from those interviewed:\n\\[ 226 \\times 0.25 = 56.5 \\]\n\nSince the number of people must be a whole number, we round 56.5 to 57 (assuming rounding to the nearest whole number is appropriate in this context).\n\nFinally, calculate the number of job offers accepted:\n\\[ 57 \\times \\frac{1}{3} = 19 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 19.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 19.0, "ground_truth": 19.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 475 * 0.48 = 228 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 228 * 0.25 = 57 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 57 * 1/3 = 19 people\n#### 19"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 942, "question": "224 people apply for a job at Apple. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 25% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, three-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 224 people apply for a job at Apple. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 25% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, three-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the steps sequentially and calculate the number of people at each stage. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Total applicants**: 224 people\n2. **Interviews**: Only 50% of the applicants receive interviews.\n   - Number of applicants receiving interviews = 224 * 0.50\n3. **Job offers**: 25% of those who receive interviews get a job offer.\n   - Number of applicants receiving job offers = (224 * 0.50) * 0.25\n4. **Acceptance**: Three-sixths of those who receive a job offer accept the position.\n   - Number of people who accept the position = (224 * 0.50 * 0.25) * (3/6)\n\nNow, let's calculate each step:\n\n1. Number of applicants receiving interviews:\n   \\[\n   224 \\times 0.50 = 112\n   \\]\n\n2. Number of applicants receiving job offers:\n   \\[\n   112 \\times 0.25 = 28\n   \\]\n\n3. Number of people who accept the position:\n   \\[\n   28 \\times \\frac{3}{6} = 28 \\times 0.5 = 14\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 14.\n</think>\n<answer>14 people accept the position.</answer>", "model_answer": 14.0, "ground_truth": 14.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 224 * 0.5 = 112 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 112 * 0.25 = 28 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 28 * 1/2 = 14 people\n#### 14"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 943, "question": "384 people apply for a job at Microsoft. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 50% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, one-half of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 384 people apply for a job at Microsoft. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 50% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, one-half of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the steps sequentially and calculate the number of people at each stage. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Total applicants**: 384 people\n2. **Interviews**: Only 50% of the applicants receive interviews.\n   - Number of applicants receiving interviews = 384 * 0.50\n3. **Job offers**: 50% of those who receive interviews get a job offer.\n   - Number of applicants receiving job offers = (384 * 0.50) * 0.50\n4. **Acceptance**: Half of those who receive a job offer accept the position.\n   - Number of people who accept the position = (384 * 0.50 * 0.50) * 0.50\n\nNow, let's calculate each step:\n\n1. Number of applicants receiving interviews:\n   \\[\n   384 \\times 0.50 = 192\n   \\]\n\n2. Number of applicants receiving job offers:\n   \\[\n   192 \\times 0.50 = 96\n   \\]\n\n3. Number of people who accept the position:\n   \\[\n   96 \\times 0.50 = 48\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 48.\n</think>\n<answer>48 people accept the position.</answer>", "model_answer": 48.0, "ground_truth": 48.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 384 * 0.5 = 192 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 192 * 0.5 = 96 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 96 * 1/2 = 48 people\n#### 48"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 944, "question": "380 people apply for a job at Netflix. Of the people that apply, only 30% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 50% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, two-thirds of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 380 people apply for a job at Netflix. Of the people that apply, only 30% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 50% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, two-thirds of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the steps sequentially and calculate the number of people at each stage. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Total applicants**: 380 people\n2. **Interviews**: 30% of the applicants receive interviews.\n   - Interviews = 380 * 0.30\n3. **Job offers**: 50% of those who receive interviews get a job offer.\n   - Job offers = Interviews * 0.50\n4. **Acceptance**: Two-thirds of those who receive a job offer accept the position.\n   - Acceptances = Job offers * (2/3)\n\nLet's calculate each step:\n\n1. Interviews:\n   - Interviews = 380 * 0.30 = 114 people\n\n2. Job offers:\n   - Job offers = 114 * 0.50 = 57 people\n\n3. Acceptances:\n   - Acceptances = 57 * (2/3) = 38 people\n\nSo, the number of people who accept the position is 38.\n</think>\n<answer>\n38 people accept the position.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 38.0, "ground_truth": 38.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 380 * 0.3 = 114 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 114 * 0.5 = 57 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 57 * 2/3 = 38 people\n#### 38"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 945, "question": "250 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 26% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 40% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, two-fourths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 250 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 26% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 40% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, two-fourths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach, breaking down the percentages and applying them sequentially to the total number of applicants. Let's start with the given information:\n1. Total applicants: 250\n2. Percentage receiving interviews: 26%\n3. Percentage receiving job offers from those who received interviews: 40%\n4. Percentage of those who receive job offers who accept: 2/4 (which simplifies to 50%)\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of applicants who receive interviews:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants receiving interviews} = 250 \\times 0.26 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of applicants who receive job offers from those who received interviews:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants receiving job offers} = \\text{Applicants receiving interviews} \\times 0.40 \\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the number of applicants who accept the job offer:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants who accept the position} = \\text{Applicants receiving job offers} \\times 0.50 \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step-by-step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate the number of applicants who receive interviews:\n\\[ 250 \\times 0.26 = 65 \\]\n\nNext, calculate the number of applicants who receive job offers:\n\\[ 65 \\times 0.40 = 26 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate the number of applicants who accept the job offer:\n\\[ 26 \\times 0.50 = 13 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 13.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 13.0, "ground_truth": 13.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 250 * 0.26 = 65 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 65 * 0.4 = 26 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 26 * 1/2 = 13 people\n#### 13"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 946, "question": "500 people apply for a job at Microsoft. Of the people that apply, only 27% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 20% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, four-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 500 people apply for a job at Microsoft. Of the people that apply, only 27% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 20% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, four-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow a step-by-step approach, breaking down the percentages and applying them sequentially to the total number of applicants. Let's start with the given information:\n1. Total applicants: 500\n2. Percentage receiving interviews: 27%\n3. Percentage receiving job offers from those interviewed: 20%\n4. Percentage accepting job offers: 4/6 (which simplifies to 2/3)\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of applicants who receive interviews:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants receiving interviews} = 500 \\times 0.27 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of applicants who receive job offers from those interviewed:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants receiving job offers} = (\\text{Applicants receiving interviews}) \\times 0.20 \\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the number of applicants who accept the job offer:\n\\[ \\text{Applicants accepting the job offer} = (\\text{Applicants receiving job offers}) \\times \\frac{2}{3} \\]\n\nLet's perform these calculations step-by-step.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, calculate the number of applicants receiving interviews:\n\\[ 500 \\times 0.27 = 135 \\]\n\nNext, calculate the number of applicants receiving job offers from those interviewed:\n\\[ 135 \\times 0.20 = 27 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate the number of applicants accepting the job offer:\n\\[ 27 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 18 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 18.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 18.0, "ground_truth": 18.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 500 * 0.27 = 135 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 135 * 0.2 = 27 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 27 * 2/3 = 18 people\n#### 18"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 947, "question": "550 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 24% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, one-half of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 550 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 24% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, one-half of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the steps sequentially and calculate the number of people at each stage. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Total applicants**: 550 people\n2. **Interviews**: Only 50% of the applicants receive interviews.\n   - Number of applicants receiving interviews = 550 * 0.50\n3. **Job offers**: Of those who receive interviews, 24% receive a job offer.\n   - Number of applicants receiving job offers = (550 * 0.50) * 0.24\n4. **Acceptance**: Of those who receive a job offer, one-half of the people accept the position.\n   - Number of people who accept the position = (550 * 0.50 * 0.24) * 0.50\n\nNow, let's perform the calculations:\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet's calculate each step:\n\n1. Number of applicants receiving interviews:\n   \\[\n   550 \\times 0.50 = 275\n   \\]\n\n2. Number of applicants receiving job offers:\n   \\[\n   275 \\times 0.24 = 66\n   \\]\n\n3. Number of people who accept the position:\n   \\[\n   66 \\times 0.50 = 33\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 33.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 33.0, "ground_truth": 33.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 550 * 0.5 = 275 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 275 * 0.24 = 66 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 66 * 1/2 = 33 people\n#### 33"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 948, "question": "1000 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 39% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, three-fifths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 1000 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 50% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 39% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, three-fifths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given percentages step by step and calculate the number of people at each stage. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Total applicants**: 1000 people\n2. **Interviews**: 50% of the applicants receive interviews\n3. **Job offers**: 39% of those who receive interviews get a job offer\n4. **Acceptance**: Three-fifths of those who receive a job offer accept the position\n\nLet's calculate each step:\n\n1. **Interviews**:\n   - 50% of 1000 people receive interviews.\n   - Interviews = 0.50 * 1000 = 500 people\n\n2. **Job offers**:\n   - 39% of the 500 people who receive interviews get a job offer.\n   - Job offers = 0.39 * 500 = 195 people\n\n3. **Acceptance**:\n   - Three-fifths of the 195 people who receive a job offer accept the position.\n   - Acceptance = (3/5) * 195\n\nNow, let's calculate the acceptance:\n   - Acceptance = (3/5) * 195 = 3 * 39 = 117 people\n\nTherefore, the number of people who accept the position is 117.\n</think>\n<answer>\n117 people accept the position.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 117.0, "ground_truth": 117.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 1000 * 0.5 = 500 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 500 * 0.39 = 195 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 195 * 3/5 = 117 people\n#### 117"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 949, "question": "600 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 10% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 10% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, five-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. 600 people apply for a job at Amazon. Of the people that apply, only 10% receive interviews. Of those who receive interviews, 10% receive a job offer. Of those who receive a job offer, five-sixths of the people accept the position. How many people accept the position?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the given percentages step by step and calculate the number of people at each stage. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Total applicants**: 600 people\n2. **Interviews**: 10% of 600 people receive interviews.\n3. **Job offers**: 10% of those who receive interviews get job offers.\n4. **Acceptance**: 5/6 of those who receive a job offer accept the position.\n\nLet's calculate each step:\n\n1. **Interviews**:\n   - 10% of 600 people receive interviews.\n   - \\( 0.10 \\times 600 = 60 \\) people receive interviews.\n\n2. **Job offers**:\n   - 10% of those who receive interviews get job offers.\n   - \\( 0.10 \\times 60 = 6 \\) people receive job offers.\n\n3. **Acceptance**:\n   - 5/6 of those who receive a job offer accept the position.\n   - \\( \\frac{5}{6} \\times 6 = 5 \\) people accept the position.\n\nSo, the number of people who accept the position is 5.\n\nLet's verify the calculations:\n- 10% of 600 = 60\n- 10% of 60 = 6\n- 5/6 of 6 = 5\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n<answer>\n5 people accept the position.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 5.0, "ground_truth": 5.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The number of people that receive interviews is 600 * 0.1 = 60 people\nThe number of people that receive a job offer is 60 * 0.1 = 6 people\nThe number of people that accept the position is 6 * 5/6 = 5 people\n#### 5"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 950, "question": "There are 9 students playing basketball and six times that number playing volleyball. There are 13 boys and 14 girls playing football. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 9 students playing basketball and six times that number playing volleyball. There are 13 boys and 14 girls playing football. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller parts and calculate the number of students in each sport group. We'll then sum these numbers to find the total number of students. Here's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. **Basketball Students**: The problem states there are 9 students playing basketball.\n2. **Volleyball Students**: It's mentioned that there are six times as many students playing volleyball as there are playing basketball. Therefore, we calculate the number of volleyball players as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Volleyball Students} = 6 \\times \\text{Basketball Students} = 6 \\times 9 = 54\n   \\]\n3. **Football Students**: The problem provides the number of boys and girls playing football. We need to sum these to find the total number of football players:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Football Students} = \\text{Number of Boys} + \\text{Number of Girls} = 13 + 14 = 27\n   \\]\n4. **Total Students**: Finally, we sum the number of students in each sport to find the total number of students:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Students} = \\text{Basketball Students} + \\text{Volleyball Students} + \\text{Football Students} = 9 + 54 + 27\n   \\]\n   Let's perform the addition:\n   \\[\n   9 + 54 = 63\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   63 + 27 = 90\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of students is 90.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe total number of students is 90.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 90.0, "ground_truth": 90.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 9 x 6 = 54 students playing volleyball.\nThere are 13 + 14 = 27 students playing football.\nIn total there are 9 + 54 + 27 = 90 students.\n#### 90"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 951, "question": "There are 11 students playing basketball and seven times that number playing badminton. There are 15 boys and 10 girls playing football. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 11 students playing basketball and seven times that number playing badminton. There are 15 boys and 10 girls playing football. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to calculate the total number of students participating in each sport and then sum them up. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Basketball Students**: We are given that there are 11 students playing basketball.\n2. **Badminton Students**: The problem states that there are seven times as many students playing badminton as those playing basketball. Therefore, we calculate:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Badminton Students} = 7 \\times 11 = 77\n   \\]\n3. **Football Students**: We are given that there are 15 boys and 10 girls playing football. Therefore, the total number of football students is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Football Students} = 15 + 10 = 25\n   \\]\n4. **Total Students**: To find the total number of students participating in all sports, we sum the students from each sport:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Students} = \\text{Basketball Students} + \\text{Badminton Students} + \\text{Football Students}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values we calculated:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Students} = 11 + 77 + 25\n   \\]\n   Adding these together:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Students} = 113\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of students participating in all sports is 113.\n</think>\n<answer>\n113\n</answer>", "model_answer": 113.0, "ground_truth": 113.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 11 x 7 = 77 students playing badminton.\nThere are 15 + 10 = 25 students playing football.\nIn total there are 11 + 77 + 25 = 113 students.\n#### 113"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 952, "question": "There are 17 students playing football and ten times that number playing table tennis. There are 11 boys and 18 girls playing basketball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 17 students playing football and ten times that number playing table tennis. There are 11 boys and 18 girls playing basketball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to calculate the total number of students participating in each sport and then sum them up. We'll break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Football Players**: The problem states there are 17 students playing football.\n2. **Table Tennis Players**: The number of students playing table tennis is ten times the number of football players. Therefore, we calculate:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times 17 = 170\n   \\]\n   So, there are 170 students playing table tennis.\n3. **Basketball Players**: The problem states there are 11 boys and 18 girls playing basketball. Therefore, the total number of basketball players is:\n   \\[\n   11 + 18 = 29\n   \\]\n   So, there are 29 students playing basketball.\n4. **Total Students**: To find the total number of students participating in any of the sports, we sum the number of students in each sport:\n   \\[\n   17 \\text{ (football)} + 170 \\text{ (table tennis)} + 29 \\text{ (basketball)} = 216\n   \\]\n\nThus, the total number of students is 216.\n</think>\n<answer>216</answer>", "model_answer": 216.0, "ground_truth": 216.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 17 x 10 = 170 students playing table tennis.\nThere are 11 + 18 = 29 students playing basketball.\nIn total there are 17 + 170 + 29 = 216 students.\n#### 216"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 953, "question": "There are 4 students playing volleyball and seven times that number playing table tennis. There are 20 boys and 28 girls playing basketball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 4 students playing volleyball and seven times that number playing table tennis. There are 20 boys and 28 girls playing basketball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller steps and calculate the number of students in each sport group. We'll then sum these numbers to find the total number of students.\n\n1. **Calculate the number of students playing table tennis:**\n   - We know that there are 4 students playing volleyball.\n   - The number of students playing table tennis is seven times the number of volleyball players.\n   - Therefore, the number of students playing table tennis = 4 * 7 = 28.\n\n2. **Calculate the total number of students playing volleyball and table tennis:**\n   - Number of volleyball players = 4\n   - Number of table tennis players = 28\n   - Total students in volleyball and table tennis = 4 + 28 = 32.\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of students playing basketball:**\n   - We know that there are 20 boys and 28 girls playing basketball.\n   - Total students playing basketball = 20 + 28 = 48.\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of students playing in all groups:**\n   - Total students in volleyball and table tennis = 32\n   - Total students in basketball = 48\n   - Total students = 32 + 48 = 80.\n\nTherefore, the total number of students is 80.\n</think>\n<answer>80</answer>", "model_answer": 80.0, "ground_truth": 80.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 4 x 7 = 28 students playing table tennis.\nThere are 20 + 28 = 48 students playing basketball.\nIn total there are 4 + 28 + 48 = 80 students.\n#### 80"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 954, "question": "There are 12 students playing volleyball and three times that number playing basketball. There are 12 boys and 14 girls playing football. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 12 students playing volleyball and three times that number playing basketball. There are 12 boys and 14 girls playing football. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller parts and calculate the number of students in each sport group. We'll then sum these numbers to find the total number of students. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Volleyball Students**: The problem states there are 12 students playing volleyball.\n2. **Basketball Students**: It's mentioned that there are three times as many students playing basketball as there are playing volleyball. Therefore, we calculate the number of basketball students as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Basketball Students} = 3 \\times \\text{Volleyball Students} = 3 \\times 12 = 36\n   \\]\n3. **Football Students**: The problem provides the number of boys and girls playing football directly:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Football Boys} = 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Football Girls} = 14\n   \\]\n   To find the total number of football students, we add the number of boys and girls:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Football Students} = \\text{Football Boys} + \\text{Football Girls} = 12 + 14 = 26\n   \\]\n4. **Total Students**: To find the total number of students participating in all sports, we sum the students from each sport:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Students} = \\text{Volleyball Students} + \\text{Basketball Students} + \\text{Football Students}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values we calculated:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Students} = 12 + 36 + 26\n   \\]\n   Adding these numbers together:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Students} = 74\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of students participating in the sports is 74.\n</think>\n<answer>74</answer>", "model_answer": 74.0, "ground_truth": 74.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 12 x 3 = 36 students playing basketball.\nThere are 12 + 14 = 26 students playing football.\nIn total there are 12 + 36 + 26 = 74 students.\n#### 74"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 955, "question": "There are 15 students playing football and five times that number playing badminton. There are 13 boys and 23 girls playing volleyball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 15 students playing football and five times that number playing badminton. There are 13 boys and 23 girls playing volleyball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to calculate the total number of students participating in each sport and then sum them up. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Football Players**: We are given that there are 15 students playing football.\n2. **Badminton Players**: The problem states that there are five times as many students playing badminton as those playing football. Therefore, the number of badminton players is:\n   \\[\n   5 \\times 15 = 75\n   \\]\n3. **Volleyball Players**: We are given that there are 13 boys and 23 girls playing volleyball. Therefore, the total number of volleyball players is:\n   \\[\n   13 + 23 = 36\n   \\]\n\nNow, we sum up the number of students playing each sport to find the total number of students:\n\n\\[\n15 \\text{ (football)} + 75 \\text{ (badminton)} + 36 \\text{ (volleyball)} = 126\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of students is 126.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe total number of students participating in the sports is 126.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 126.0, "ground_truth": 126.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 15 x 5 = 75 students playing badminton.\nThere are 13 + 23 = 36 students playing volleyball.\nIn total there are 15 + 75 + 36 = 126 students.\n#### 126"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 956, "question": "There are 6 students playing table tennis and two times that number playing badminton. There are 18 boys and 20 girls playing basketball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 6 students playing table tennis and two times that number playing badminton. There are 18 boys and 20 girls playing basketball. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller parts and calculate the number of students in each sport group. We'll then sum these numbers to find the total number of students. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n1. First, let's calculate the number of students playing table tennis:\n   - There are 6 students playing table tennis.\n2. Next, we need to find out how many students are playing badminton:\n   - The problem states that there are two times the number of students playing table tennis playing badminton.\n   - Therefore, the number of students playing badminton = 2 * 6 = 12 students.\n3. Now, let's calculate the number of students playing basketball:\n   - The problem states that there are 18 boys and 20 girls playing basketball.\n   - Therefore, the total number of students playing basketball = 18 + 20 = 38 students.\n4. Finally, we need to sum the number of students in each sport group to find the total number of students:\n   - Total number of students = Students playing table tennis + Students playing badminton + Students playing basketball\n   - Total number of students = 6 + 12 + 38\n   - Total number of students = 56\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe total number of students is 56.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 56.0, "ground_truth": 56.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 6 x 2 = 12 students playing badminton.\nThere are 18 + 20 = 38 students playing basketball.\nIn total there are 6 + 12 + 38 = 56 students.\n#### 56"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 957, "question": "There are 14 students playing basketball and nine times that number playing football. There are 23 boys and 26 girls playing table tennis. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 14 students playing basketball and nine times that number playing football. There are 23 boys and 26 girls playing table tennis. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the number of students playing football.\n2. Sum the number of students playing basketball and football.\n3. Add the number of boys and girls playing table tennis to the total from step 2.\n4. Ensure that each student is counted only once.\n\nLet's go through each step in detail:\n\n1. Number of students playing football:\n   - Given that there are 14 students playing basketball and nine times that number playing football.\n   - Number of students playing football = 9 × 14 = 126 students.\n\n2. Total number of students playing basketball and football:\n   - Students playing basketball = 14\n   - Students playing football = 126\n   - Total = 14 + 126 = 140 students.\n\n3. Number of students playing table tennis:\n   - Given that there are 23 boys and 26 girls.\n   - Total students playing table tennis = 23 + 26 = 49 students.\n\n4. Total number of students:\n   - Students playing basketball and football = 140\n   - Students playing table tennis = 49\n   - Total students = 140 + 49 = 189 students.\n\nTherefore, the total number of students is 189.\n</think>\n<answer>189</answer>", "model_answer": 189.0, "ground_truth": 189.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 14 x 9 = 126 students playing football.\nThere are 23 + 26 = 49 students playing table tennis.\nIn total there are 14 + 126 + 49 = 189 students.\n#### 189"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 958, "question": "There are 8 students playing badminton and two times that number playing basketball. There are 30 boys and 11 girls playing table tennis. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 8 students playing badminton and two times that number playing basketball. There are 30 boys and 11 girls playing table tennis. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller parts and calculate the number of students in each sport. We'll then sum these numbers to find the total number of students. Let's start with the badminton players:\n1. We are given that there are 8 students playing badminton.\n2. The number of students playing basketball is twice the number of badminton players, so we calculate:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 8 = 16 \\text{ students playing basketball}\n   \\]\nNext, we'll calculate the number of students playing table tennis:\n3. We are given that there are 30 boys and 11 girls playing table tennis. Therefore, the total number of table tennis players is:\n   \\[\n   30 + 11 = 41 \\text{ students playing table tennis}\n   \\]\nNow, we sum the number of students in each sport to find the total number of students:\n4. Total number of students:\n   \\[\n   8 \\text{ (badminton)} + 16 \\text{ (basketball)} + 41 \\text{ (table tennis)} = 65 \\text{ students}\n   \\]\nSo, the total number of students is 65.\n</think>\n<answer>\n65 students in total.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 65.0, "ground_truth": 65.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 8 x 2 = 16 students playing basketball.\nThere are 30 + 11 = 41 students playing table tennis.\nIn total there are 8 + 16 + 41 = 65 students.\n#### 65"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 959, "question": "There are 18 students playing volleyball and seven times that number playing football. There are 23 boys and 28 girls playing badminton. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. There are 18 students playing volleyball and seven times that number playing football. There are 23 boys and 28 girls playing badminton. If each student only participates in one group, how many students are there in total?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the number of students playing football.\n2. Sum the number of students playing volleyball, football, and badminton.\n3. Verify the total number of students.\n\nFirst, we know that there are 18 students playing volleyball. The number of students playing football is seven times that number:\n\\[ \\text{Number of students playing football} = 7 \\times 18 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of students playing football:\n\\[ 7 \\times 18 = 126 \\]\n\nNow, we know the number of students playing each sport:\n- Volleyball: 18 students\n- Football: 126 students\n- Badminton: 23 boys + 28 girls = 51 students\n\nTo find the total number of students, we sum the students from each sport:\n\\[ \\text{Total number of students} = 18 + 126 + 51 \\]\n\nLet's perform the addition:\n\\[ 18 + 126 = 144 \\]\n\\[ 144 + 51 = 195 \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of students is:\n\\[ \\boxed{195} \\]\n</think>\n<answer>\n195\n</answer>", "model_answer": 195.0, "ground_truth": 195.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "There are 18 x 7 = 126 students playing football.\nThere are 23 + 28 = 51 students playing badminton.\nIn total there are 18 + 126 + 51 = 195 students.\n#### 195"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 960, "question": "Gabrielle's dad said that she had ₺120 budgeted for her birthday party. She wants to make sure she and her friends all get to play one round of laser tag, have ₺3 in pinball tokens, and get to ride the roller coaster four times. A round of laser tag is ₺5. The roller coaster cost ₺8 a ride. How many friends can she invite?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Gabrielle's dad said that she had ₺120 budgeted for her birthday party. She wants to make sure she and her friends all get to play one round of laser tag, have ₺3 in pinball tokens, and get to ride the roller coaster four times. A round of laser tag is ₺5. The roller coaster cost ₺8 a ride. How many friends can she invite?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller parts and calculate the total cost for the activities Gabrielle wants to do. We know her total budget is ₺120. We need to subtract the cost of laser tag, pinball tokens, and roller coaster rides from this budget to find out how much money is left for her friends. Then, we can determine how many friends she can invite based on the remaining budget.\n</think>\n<answer>\nFirst, let's calculate the cost of laser tag for Gabrielle and her friends. Since a round of laser tag costs ₺5 and she wants to play one round, the cost for laser tag is ₺5.\n\nNext, we calculate the cost of pinball tokens. Gabrielle wants to have ₺3 in pinball tokens, so the cost for pinball tokens is ₺3.\n\nNow, let's calculate the cost of the roller coaster rides. Gabrielle wants to ride the roller coaster four times, and each ride costs ₺8. Therefore, the total cost for the roller coaster rides is 4 rides * ₺8/ride = ₺32.\n\nNow we can calculate the total cost for these activities:\nTotal cost = Cost of laser tag + Cost of pinball tokens + Cost of roller coaster rides\nTotal cost = ₺5 + ₺3 + ₺32 = ₺40\n\nNow we subtract the total cost from Gabrielle's budget to find out how much money is left for her friends:\nMoney left = Total budget - Total cost\nMoney left = ₺120 - ₺40 = ₺80\n\nNow we need to determine how many friends she can invite. Since each friend will get to play one round of laser tag, have ₺3 in pinball tokens, and ride the roller coaster four times, we need to calculate the cost for one friend:\nCost for one friend = Cost of laser tag + Cost of pinball tokens + Cost of roller coaster rides\nCost for one friend = ₺5 + ₺3 + ₺32 = ₺40\n\nFinally, we divide the money left by the cost for one friend to find out how many friends she can invite:\nNumber of friends = Money left / Cost for one friend\nNumber of friends = ₺80 / ₺40 = 2\n\nGabrielle can invite 2 friends to her birthday party.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 2.0, "ground_truth": 2.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The roller coaster will cost ₺32 per person because 8 x 4 = 32\nEach person costs ₺40 because 3 + 5 + 32 = 40\n3 total people can attend because 120 / 40 = 3\nShe can invite 2 friends because 3 - 1 =2\n#### 2"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 961, "question": "Sakura's mom said that she had ₺200 budgeted for her birthday party. She wants to make sure she and her friends all get to play one round of mini-golf, have ₺3 in arcade tokens, and get to ride the go-karts five times. A round of mini-golf is ₺7. The go-karts cost ₺18 a ride. How many friends can she invite?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Sakura's mom said that she had ₺200 budgeted for her birthday party. She wants to make sure she and her friends all get to play one round of mini-golf, have ₺3 in arcade tokens, and get to ride the go-karts five times. A round of mini-golf is ₺7. The go-karts cost ₺18 a ride. How many friends can she invite?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller parts and calculate the total cost for Sakura's birthday party. We know the total budget is ₺200. We need to account for the cost of mini-golf, arcade tokens, and go-karts rides. Let's denote the number of friends as \\( n \\). Since Sakura is also attending, the total number of people is \\( n + 1 \\).\n\n1. **Cost of mini-golf**: Each round costs ₺7, and each person gets one round. So, the total cost for mini-golf is \\( 7 \\times (n + 1) \\).\n2. **Cost of arcade tokens**: Each person gets ₺3 in arcade tokens. So, the total cost for arcade tokens is \\( 3 \\times (n + 1) \\).\n3. **Cost of go-karts**: Each person gets to ride the go-karts five times, and each ride costs ₺18. So, the total cost for go-karts is \\( 18 \\times 5 \\times (n + 1) = 90 \\times (n + 1) \\).\n\nThe total cost for the party is the sum of these three costs, which should not exceed ₺200. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\\[ 7(n + 1) + 3(n + 1) + 90(n + 1) = 200 \\]\n\nWe can factor out \\( n + 1 \\) from the left side of the equation:\n\\[ (7 + 3 + 90)(n + 1) = 200 \\]\n\\[ 90(n + 1) = 200 \\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\( n + 1 \\):\n\\[ n + 1 = \\frac{200}{90} \\]\n\\[ n + 1 = \\frac{20}{9} \\]\n\\[ n + 1 \\approx 2.22 \\]\n\nSince \\( n \\) must be a whole number, we take the integer part of \\( n + 1 \\), which is 2. Therefore, \\( n = 2 - 1 = 1 \\).\n\nSo, Sakura can invite 1 friend.\n</think>\n<answer>\nSakura can invite 1 friend to her birthday party.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 1.0, "ground_truth": 1.0, "is_correct": true, "original_answer_text": "The go-karts will cost ₺90 per person because 18 x 5 = 90\nEach person costs ₺100 because 3 + 7 + 90 = 100\n2 total people can attend because 200 / 100 = 2\nShe can invite 1 friends because 2 - 1 =1\n#### 1"}
{"dataset": "GSM_Original", "item_id": 962, "question": "Uma's mom said that she had ¥220 budgeted for her birthday party. She wants to make sure she and her friends all get to play one round of bowling, have ¥6 in game room tokens, and get to ride the bumper cars nine times. A round of bowling is ¥5. The bumper cars cost ¥11 a ride. How many friends can she invite?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Uma's mom said that she had ¥220 budgeted for her birthday party. She wants to make sure she and her friends all get to play one round of bowling, have ¥6 in game room tokens, and get to ride the bumper cars nine times. A round of bowling is ¥5. The bumper cars cost ¥11 a ride. How many friends can she invite?", "model_response": "<think>\nTo solve this problem, we need to break it down into smaller parts and calculate the total cost for Uma's birthday party. We know the total budget is ¥220. We need to account for the cost of bowling, game room tokens, and bumper car rides. Let's denote the number of friends Uma can invite as \\( f \\).\n\n1. **Bowling Cost**: Uma and her friends will each play one round of bowling. If Uma invites \\( f \\) friends, there will be \\( f + 1 \\) people playing bowling. The cost for bowling is ¥5 per round. So, the total cost for bowling is:\n   \\[\n   5 \\times (f + 1)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Game Room Tokens**: Uma wants to have ¥6 in game room tokens. So, the cost for game room tokens is:\n   \\[\n   6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bumper Cars**: Uma wants to ride the bumper cars nine times. The cost for each ride is ¥11. So, the total cost for bumper cars is:\n   \\[\n   11 \\times 9 = 99\n   \\]\n\nNow, we can set up the equation for the total cost:\n\\[\n5 \\times (f + 1) + 6 + 99 = 220\n\\]\n\nLet's simplify and solve for \\( f \\):\n\\[\n5f + 5 + 6 + 99 = 220\n\\]\n\\[\n5f + 110 = 220\n\\]\n\\[\n5f = 220 - 110\n\\]\n\\[\n5f = 110\n\\]\n\\[\nf = \\frac{110}{5}\n\\]\n\\[\nf = 22\n\\]\n\nSo, Uma can invite 22 friends.\n</think>\n<answer>\nUma can invite 22 friends to her birthday party.\n</answer>", "model_answer": 22.0, "ground_truth": 1.0, "is_correct": false, "original_answer_text": "The bumper cars will cost ¥99 per person because 11 x 9 = 99\nEach person costs ¥110 because 6 + 5 + 99 = 110\n2 total people can attend because 220 / 110 = 2\nShe can invite 1 friends because 2 - 1 =1\n#### 1"}
